WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.452 align:middle line:90%
[SQUEAKING]

00:00:02.452 --> 00:00:02.952 align:middle line:90%


00:00:02.952 --> 00:00:03.928 align:middle line:90%
[RUSTLING]

00:00:03.928 --> 00:00:04.428 align:middle line:90%


00:00:04.428 --> 00:00:07.872 align:middle line:90%
[CLICKING]

00:00:07.872 --> 00:00:16.760 align:middle line:90%


00:00:16.760 --> 00:00:20.160 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: Today, we
have a special guest

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:23.080 align:middle line:90%
lecturer, Professor Andrew Lo.

00:00:23.080 --> 00:00:26.140 align:middle line:84%
A lot of you may have
already known Professor Lo,

00:00:26.140 --> 00:00:29.800 align:middle line:84%
but I still will make
a quick introduction.

00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:36.760 align:middle line:84%
So Professor Lo is professor at
the MIT Sloan Business School

00:00:36.760 --> 00:00:38.040 align:middle line:90%
for many years.

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:42.760 align:middle line:84%
He's also the director of
the Laboratory for Financial

00:00:42.760 --> 00:00:48.600 align:middle line:84%
Engineering and a
principal investigator

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:52.520 align:middle line:84%
with MIT CSAIL, which is the
Computer Science and Artificial

00:00:52.520 --> 00:00:55.400 align:middle line:90%
Intelligence Lab.

00:00:55.400 --> 00:01:03.150 align:middle line:84%
He also has many other
active involvement with, say,

00:01:03.150 --> 00:01:04.489 align:middle line:90%
just a couple of examples.

00:01:04.489 --> 00:01:08.670 align:middle line:84%
Santa Fe Institute
and NBER, which

00:01:08.670 --> 00:01:13.710 align:middle line:84%
is a National Bureau
of Economic Research.

00:01:13.710 --> 00:01:17.450 align:middle line:84%
And Professor Lo's research
areas are very broad too.

00:01:17.450 --> 00:01:22.150 align:middle line:84%
So I think it will be too long
for me to read through them.

00:01:22.150 --> 00:01:24.190 align:middle line:84%
I think instead of
that, I will pass it

00:01:24.190 --> 00:01:27.690 align:middle line:84%
on to Professor Lo
for today's lecture.

00:01:27.690 --> 00:01:28.690 align:middle line:90%
ANDREW W. LO: All right.

00:01:28.690 --> 00:01:28.890 align:middle line:90%
Great.

00:01:28.890 --> 00:01:30.110 align:middle line:90%
Thanks very much, Jake.

00:01:30.110 --> 00:01:35.190 align:middle line:84%
Thank you, Peter, for
inviting me to 18.642.

00:01:35.190 --> 00:01:36.130 align:middle line:90%
Happy to be here.

00:01:36.130 --> 00:01:40.310 align:middle line:84%
And I'm going to be talking
to you about some big ideas,

00:01:40.310 --> 00:01:43.430 align:middle line:84%
and some big
challenges, particularly

00:01:43.430 --> 00:01:48.110 align:middle line:84%
for your generation, because
our generation has created

00:01:48.110 --> 00:01:50.470 align:middle line:84%
the problems that
your generation is

00:01:50.470 --> 00:01:51.910 align:middle line:90%
going to need to deal with.

00:01:51.910 --> 00:01:54.070 align:middle line:84%
And so what I'm
hoping to do today

00:01:54.070 --> 00:01:56.790 align:middle line:84%
is also to give you some
of the tools by which you

00:01:56.790 --> 00:01:59.700 align:middle line:84%
can deal with some of those
problems, problems like climate

00:01:59.700 --> 00:02:02.820 align:middle line:84%
change, like
dealing with cancer,

00:02:02.820 --> 00:02:07.660 align:middle line:84%
various kinds of energy demands
that are growing exponentially

00:02:07.660 --> 00:02:09.419 align:middle line:90%
even as we speak.

00:02:09.419 --> 00:02:12.900 align:middle line:84%
So by background, I'm
a financial economist

00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:15.540 align:middle line:90%
by training and trade.

00:02:15.540 --> 00:02:19.085 align:middle line:84%
Got my PhD at Harvard in
economics, financial economics.

00:02:19.085 --> 00:02:20.960 align:middle line:84%
Then ended up teaching
at the Wharton School.

00:02:20.960 --> 00:02:23.200 align:middle line:84%
And for the last 35 years,
I've been teaching at MIT

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:25.220 align:middle line:84%
at the Sloan School
in the finance group.

00:02:25.220 --> 00:02:28.660 align:middle line:84%
And when I teach my
first-year MBAs in finance,

00:02:28.660 --> 00:02:31.220 align:middle line:84%
I start off by
telling them, finance

00:02:31.220 --> 00:02:33.220 align:middle line:84%
is the most important
subject you will ever

00:02:33.220 --> 00:02:35.500 align:middle line:90%
take at a business school.

00:02:35.500 --> 00:02:39.540 align:middle line:84%
And I acknowledge that
my other colleagues

00:02:39.540 --> 00:02:43.500 align:middle line:84%
in departments like accounting,
and marketing, operations

00:02:43.500 --> 00:02:47.500 align:middle line:84%
research, they may feel the
same way about their subject.

00:02:47.500 --> 00:02:49.840 align:middle line:84%
But the key difference
is that in my case,

00:02:49.840 --> 00:02:51.540 align:middle line:90%
I happen to be right.

00:02:51.540 --> 00:02:54.640 align:middle line:84%
And the reason is because
no matter what you do,

00:02:54.640 --> 00:02:56.610 align:middle line:84%
no matter what
field you go into,

00:02:56.610 --> 00:02:59.710 align:middle line:84%
no matter what industry
you choose for your job,

00:02:59.710 --> 00:03:02.170 align:middle line:84%
at some point or
another, when you want

00:03:02.170 --> 00:03:04.210 align:middle line:84%
to do something really
important, really

00:03:04.210 --> 00:03:07.810 align:middle line:84%
transformative, you
will need money.

00:03:07.810 --> 00:03:10.550 align:middle line:84%
And at that point, you will
be speaking my language.

00:03:10.550 --> 00:03:14.130 align:middle line:84%
You will need to understand the
basics of financial analysis,

00:03:14.130 --> 00:03:16.650 align:middle line:84%
things like cost of
capital, net present value

00:03:16.650 --> 00:03:18.570 align:middle line:90%
calculations, and so on.

00:03:18.570 --> 00:03:21.710 align:middle line:84%
So in this very short
and compressed lecture,

00:03:21.710 --> 00:03:23.930 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to give you
an exposure to all

00:03:23.930 --> 00:03:27.050 align:middle line:84%
of those concepts in the
context of dealing with some

00:03:27.050 --> 00:03:28.630 align:middle line:90%
of society's biggest issues.

00:03:28.630 --> 00:03:31.110 align:middle line:84%
And I'm going to focus
on two in particular.

00:03:31.110 --> 00:03:33.170 align:middle line:84%
One is in biomedicine,
and the other

00:03:33.170 --> 00:03:38.010 align:middle line:84%
is going to be in energy,
particularly fusion energy.

00:03:38.010 --> 00:03:40.810 align:middle line:84%
So let me first start
with an observation

00:03:40.810 --> 00:03:42.230 align:middle line:90%
on the first set of examples.

00:03:42.230 --> 00:03:45.510 align:middle line:84%
And that is that biomedicine
is at an inflection point.

00:03:45.510 --> 00:03:48.650 align:middle line:84%
Now some of you who are
biology majors, you know this.

00:03:48.650 --> 00:03:51.830 align:middle line:84%
Actually, do we have any biology
majors here in this class?

00:03:51.830 --> 00:03:53.940 align:middle line:84%
Anybody doing anything
in bioengineering?

00:03:53.940 --> 00:03:55.600 align:middle line:90%
How about chemical engineering?

00:03:55.600 --> 00:03:57.040 align:middle line:90%
Chemistry?

00:03:57.040 --> 00:03:57.920 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:03:57.920 --> 00:03:58.628 align:middle line:90%
ANDREW W. LO: OK.

00:03:58.628 --> 00:03:59.440 align:middle line:90%
All right.

00:03:59.440 --> 00:04:01.340 align:middle line:84%
Well, people do math
and other subjects.

00:04:01.340 --> 00:04:07.540 align:middle line:84%
But anyway, it turns out
that in biomedicine, things

00:04:07.540 --> 00:04:09.040 align:middle line:84%
are different than
they've ever been

00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:10.540 align:middle line:90%
in the history of the field.

00:04:10.540 --> 00:04:13.680 align:middle line:84%
And I'm going to give
you two examples of that.

00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:16.399 align:middle line:84%
The first example has to do
with these two kids, Caroline

00:04:16.399 --> 00:04:17.839 align:middle line:90%
and Cole Carper.

00:04:17.839 --> 00:04:21.880 align:middle line:84%
They were born with a rare
condition known as Leber's

00:04:21.880 --> 00:04:23.880 align:middle line:90%
congenital amaurosis.

00:04:23.880 --> 00:04:28.080 align:middle line:84%
It's a single-gene mutation,
a typo in your DNA,

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:31.180 align:middle line:84%
that causes blindness
starting at birth.

00:04:31.180 --> 00:04:33.280 align:middle line:84%
You're born with a
normal pair of eyes,

00:04:33.280 --> 00:04:35.280 align:middle line:84%
but this mutation
prevents your eyes

00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:38.360 align:middle line:84%
from producing a very
important protein.

00:04:38.360 --> 00:04:40.340 align:middle line:84%
And without the protein,
you start to go blind.

00:04:40.340 --> 00:04:41.900 align:middle line:84%
So by the time you're
six months old,

00:04:41.900 --> 00:04:45.520 align:middle line:84%
you are legally blind
from that point on.

00:04:45.520 --> 00:04:50.080 align:middle line:84%
And so these two kids were part
of a clinical trial in 2016,

00:04:50.080 --> 00:04:53.180 align:middle line:84%
started by a Philadelphia-based
drug company called

00:04:53.180 --> 00:04:57.380 align:middle line:84%
Spark Therapeutics, that used
a really remarkable therapy

00:04:57.380 --> 00:05:00.900 align:middle line:84%
to try to deal with
their genetic mutation.

00:05:00.900 --> 00:05:03.780 align:middle line:84%
It's a treatment
known as gene therapy.

00:05:03.780 --> 00:05:06.480 align:middle line:90%
And the way it works is this.

00:05:06.480 --> 00:05:09.060 align:middle line:84%
You take the correct
version of the gene

00:05:09.060 --> 00:05:11.860 align:middle line:90%
that these two kids don't have.

00:05:11.860 --> 00:05:14.740 align:middle line:90%
You insert it into a virus.

00:05:14.740 --> 00:05:17.220 align:middle line:84%
And then you inject
the virus into the back

00:05:17.220 --> 00:05:19.860 align:middle line:90%
of the eyes of these two kids.

00:05:19.860 --> 00:05:22.660 align:middle line:90%
One-time injection.

00:05:22.660 --> 00:05:27.020 align:middle line:84%
And what happens is the
virus infects the cells,

00:05:27.020 --> 00:05:31.100 align:middle line:84%
and picks the correct
gene, and swaps it

00:05:31.100 --> 00:05:34.940 align:middle line:84%
with the incorrect
version in those cells.

00:05:34.940 --> 00:05:38.460 align:middle line:84%
And from that point
forward, these kids' cells

00:05:38.460 --> 00:05:42.740 align:middle line:84%
in the back of their eyes
start producing this protein.

00:05:42.740 --> 00:05:44.380 align:middle line:84%
And let me tell
you what Caroline

00:05:44.380 --> 00:05:49.570 align:middle line:84%
said a few days after she
received the injection.

00:05:49.570 --> 00:05:51.410 align:middle line:84%
"I went outside
when it was snowing,

00:05:51.410 --> 00:05:54.250 align:middle line:84%
and I was like, oh, I
can see the snowflakes.

00:05:54.250 --> 00:05:57.810 align:middle line:84%
It was really cool to actually
see something that I've never

00:05:57.810 --> 00:06:01.490 align:middle line:90%
seen in my life before."

00:06:01.490 --> 00:06:06.410 align:middle line:84%
One-time injection,
and now, she can see.

00:06:06.410 --> 00:06:11.130 align:middle line:84%
The second example has to
do with a disease known

00:06:11.130 --> 00:06:13.430 align:middle line:90%
as Canavan disease.

00:06:13.430 --> 00:06:17.130 align:middle line:84%
So this is also a
single-gene mutation, a typo.

00:06:17.130 --> 00:06:22.050 align:middle line:84%
And what this disease
does is pretty subtle.

00:06:22.050 --> 00:06:26.130 align:middle line:84%
It prevents your
nerve cells from being

00:06:26.130 --> 00:06:32.650 align:middle line:84%
able to maintain the myelin
sheath that basically provides

00:06:32.650 --> 00:06:35.310 align:middle line:84%
the insulation for
that nerve cell.

00:06:35.310 --> 00:06:39.330 align:middle line:84%
Now you all know that
nerve cells have a nucleus,

00:06:39.330 --> 00:06:44.330 align:middle line:84%
and they've got an axon,
right, this long string that

00:06:44.330 --> 00:06:49.520 align:middle line:84%
conducts electricity, and allows
you to basically take an impulse

00:06:49.520 --> 00:06:53.560 align:middle line:84%
and transmit it to various
parts of your body.

00:06:53.560 --> 00:06:57.140 align:middle line:84%
And the purpose of this myelin,
myelin is a kind of fat.

00:06:57.140 --> 00:07:00.640 align:middle line:84%
The purpose of this
fatty sheath that

00:07:00.640 --> 00:07:04.860 align:middle line:84%
goes on the outside of every
nerve cell is insulation.

00:07:04.860 --> 00:07:08.640 align:middle line:84%
It's like a wire that has to
have some kind of insulation

00:07:08.640 --> 00:07:13.760 align:middle line:84%
to prevent the electrical
impulses to cross

00:07:13.760 --> 00:07:15.560 align:middle line:90%
to other wires.

00:07:15.560 --> 00:07:17.540 align:middle line:84%
When that happens, you
get short circuits.

00:07:17.540 --> 00:07:20.480 align:middle line:84%
And obviously,
that's a bad thing.

00:07:20.480 --> 00:07:24.120 align:middle line:84%
Well, it turns out that kids
with this single-gene mutation,

00:07:24.120 --> 00:07:27.840 align:middle line:84%
after they're born, the
myelin sheaths in their body

00:07:27.840 --> 00:07:29.280 align:middle line:90%
begin to degrade.

00:07:29.280 --> 00:07:33.500 align:middle line:84%
And so the electrical impulses
start to short circuit.

00:07:33.500 --> 00:07:36.240 align:middle line:84%
And so you have a hard time
controlling your muscles.

00:07:36.240 --> 00:07:39.240 align:middle line:84%
And so generally, these
kids have a lifespan

00:07:39.240 --> 00:07:42.480 align:middle line:90%
of about 10 to 12 years.

00:07:42.480 --> 00:07:45.990 align:middle line:84%
This is an example
of a Canavan patient.

00:07:45.990 --> 00:07:50.710 align:middle line:84%
He's probably close
to his end of life.

00:07:50.710 --> 00:07:53.590 align:middle line:84%
They generally
don't grow beyond 15

00:07:53.590 --> 00:07:56.110 align:middle line:84%
just because it becomes
harder and harder

00:07:56.110 --> 00:07:58.470 align:middle line:84%
to control your
breathing, your heartbeat.

00:07:58.470 --> 00:08:01.470 align:middle line:84%
All of the bodily functions
that we take for granted

00:08:01.470 --> 00:08:04.430 align:middle line:84%
become much more difficult
in Canavan patients.

00:08:04.430 --> 00:08:07.650 align:middle line:84%
You'll notice that he's
leaned back on his wheelchair.

00:08:07.650 --> 00:08:11.130 align:middle line:84%
The reason is that his head
tends to be larger than normal.

00:08:11.130 --> 00:08:16.910 align:middle line:84%
This hyperencephaly,
your head gets bigger,

00:08:16.910 --> 00:08:19.230 align:middle line:84%
is part of the symptom
of the disease.

00:08:19.230 --> 00:08:22.570 align:middle line:84%
And at some point, your neck,
because there's no muscle tone,

00:08:22.570 --> 00:08:24.950 align:middle line:84%
because you can't use the
muscles because of the nerve

00:08:24.950 --> 00:08:27.790 align:middle line:84%
impulses that get
short circuited,

00:08:27.790 --> 00:08:32.130 align:middle line:84%
it's very easy to snap the neck
if you just lean over like this.

00:08:32.130 --> 00:08:34.750 align:middle line:84%
And so you have to always
be in a prone position,

00:08:34.750 --> 00:08:38.909 align:middle line:84%
and your head protected
by these cushions.

00:08:38.909 --> 00:08:41.950 align:middle line:84%
There's lots of other
terrible symptoms.

00:08:41.950 --> 00:08:43.350 align:middle line:90%
Let me tell you what they are.

00:08:43.350 --> 00:08:46.320 align:middle line:84%
Children with Canavan disease
cannot crawl, cannot walk,

00:08:46.320 --> 00:08:47.900 align:middle line:90%
cannot sit, or talk.

00:08:47.900 --> 00:08:50.120 align:middle line:84%
Over time, they may
suffer seizures,

00:08:50.120 --> 00:08:52.320 align:middle line:84%
become paralyzed,
developmentally delayed,

00:08:52.320 --> 00:08:54.720 align:middle line:84%
blind, and have
trouble swallowing.

00:08:54.720 --> 00:08:57.380 align:middle line:84%
Deafness may also
result. Many children

00:08:57.380 --> 00:08:59.200 align:middle line:90%
do not live past the age of 10.

00:08:59.200 --> 00:09:02.680 align:middle line:84%
And at present time, there is
no cure for Canavan disease.

00:09:02.680 --> 00:09:06.520 align:middle line:84%
Treatment involves
managing the symptoms.

00:09:06.520 --> 00:09:09.180 align:middle line:84%
And so the typical
Canavan patient,

00:09:09.180 --> 00:09:12.760 align:middle line:84%
the disease generally affects
you starting at birth.

00:09:12.760 --> 00:09:19.100 align:middle line:84%
But in some cases, it can get
progressively worse over time.

00:09:19.100 --> 00:09:22.600 align:middle line:84%
The doctors that know how
to diagnose this disease,

00:09:22.600 --> 00:09:24.300 align:middle line:84%
typically what they
tell their parents

00:09:24.300 --> 00:09:28.220 align:middle line:90%
is, go home and love your child.

00:09:28.220 --> 00:09:31.020 align:middle line:90%
That's their treatment.

00:09:31.020 --> 00:09:31.520 align:middle line:90%
That's it.

00:09:31.520 --> 00:09:34.060 align:middle line:90%
That's all they can do.

00:09:34.060 --> 00:09:35.700 align:middle line:84%
So it turns out
that you can treat

00:09:35.700 --> 00:09:38.860 align:middle line:84%
this disease using the same
technique, gene therapy.

00:09:38.860 --> 00:09:42.130 align:middle line:84%
Take the correct version of
the gene, put it into a virus,

00:09:42.130 --> 00:09:46.690 align:middle line:84%
inject the virus, in this case,
into the brains of these Canavan

00:09:46.690 --> 00:09:47.650 align:middle line:90%
patients.

00:09:47.650 --> 00:09:53.850 align:middle line:84%
And well, I'm going to show you
a video clip of what happens

00:09:53.850 --> 00:09:55.590 align:middle line:90%
with one particular patient.

00:09:55.590 --> 00:09:58.650 align:middle line:84%
This is patient number
three, I believe.

00:09:58.650 --> 00:10:00.770 align:middle line:84%
And what you're going to
see is a clinical trial

00:10:00.770 --> 00:10:05.210 align:middle line:84%
of the patient
before the treatment,

00:10:05.210 --> 00:10:06.430 align:middle line:90%
after she's diagnosed.

00:10:06.430 --> 00:10:09.610 align:middle line:84%
So they know that she's
got this mutation.

00:10:09.610 --> 00:10:12.330 align:middle line:84%
You can see the symptoms
that are developing.

00:10:12.330 --> 00:10:13.970 align:middle line:84%
And the frustrating
thing is that you

00:10:13.970 --> 00:10:16.550 align:middle line:90%
are born relatively normal.

00:10:16.550 --> 00:10:20.330 align:middle line:84%
But because your body can't
maintain the myelin sheaths,

00:10:20.330 --> 00:10:24.250 align:middle line:84%
you begin to degenerate
over a period of time.

00:10:24.250 --> 00:10:28.070 align:middle line:84%
So let me show you
what's going on here.

00:10:28.070 --> 00:10:31.020 align:middle line:84%
So this is June 2022 just
before the treatment.

00:10:31.020 --> 00:10:32.050 align:middle line:90%
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:10:32.050 --> 00:10:35.780 align:middle line:90%
- So this is the patient.

00:10:35.780 --> 00:10:37.030 align:middle line:90%
- You eating your blueberries?

00:10:37.030 --> 00:10:39.650 align:middle line:90%
- And she's about one year old.

00:10:39.650 --> 00:10:42.900 align:middle line:84%
And so at this age, you
should be able to move around.

00:10:42.900 --> 00:10:47.820 align:middle line:84%
But look at her hand motions,
and how jerky her control is.

00:10:47.820 --> 00:10:49.320 align:middle line:84%
It's going to take
her quite a while

00:10:49.320 --> 00:10:52.800 align:middle line:84%
just to grab one blueberry
and put it in her mouth.

00:10:52.800 --> 00:10:55.240 align:middle line:84%
So this is going to
get much, much worse

00:10:55.240 --> 00:10:59.180 align:middle line:84%
at this particular
rate of degeneration.

00:10:59.180 --> 00:10:59.860 align:middle line:90%
[END PLAYBACK]

00:10:59.860 --> 00:11:00.527 align:middle line:90%
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:11:00.527 --> 00:11:05.820 align:middle line:84%
- Now this is about six months
after that one-time injection.

00:11:05.820 --> 00:11:06.320 align:middle line:90%
- Wow.

00:11:06.320 --> 00:11:07.980 align:middle line:90%
Look at you trucking along.

00:11:07.980 --> 00:11:08.560 align:middle line:90%
Come on.

00:11:08.560 --> 00:11:12.920 align:middle line:84%
- She's actually walking, even
though it's with a walker.

00:11:12.920 --> 00:11:15.000 align:middle line:90%
She's actually able to walk.

00:11:15.000 --> 00:11:17.400 align:middle line:84%
Now you can tell that
there's still slow--

00:11:17.400 --> 00:11:19.080 align:middle line:90%
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:11:19.080 --> 00:11:22.540 align:middle line:90%
- She can't walk on her own yet.

00:11:22.540 --> 00:11:24.780 align:middle line:84%
But this is only six
months after the treatment.

00:11:24.780 --> 00:11:26.100 align:middle line:90%
OK?

00:11:26.100 --> 00:11:29.880 align:middle line:84%
Now this is just
around the same time.

00:11:29.880 --> 00:11:35.000 align:middle line:84%
She is now getting ready to
actually stand on her own.

00:11:35.000 --> 00:11:38.230 align:middle line:84%
And this uses an enormous
amount of core muscles.

00:11:38.230 --> 00:11:41.190 align:middle line:84%
You probably all know you
have to exercise your core.

00:11:41.190 --> 00:11:45.230 align:middle line:84%
So she's developing
core muscle strength.

00:11:45.230 --> 00:11:47.590 align:middle line:84%
She can't quite do it
yet, but she's close.

00:11:47.590 --> 00:11:48.630 align:middle line:90%
Very close.

00:11:48.630 --> 00:11:50.350 align:middle line:90%
And--

00:11:50.350 --> 00:11:51.670 align:middle line:90%
- There you go.

00:11:51.670 --> 00:11:52.570 align:middle line:90%
- There she goes.

00:11:52.570 --> 00:11:55.390 align:middle line:90%
She's just about to do it.

00:11:55.390 --> 00:11:55.930 align:middle line:90%
That's it.

00:11:55.930 --> 00:11:58.550 align:middle line:84%
For the very first time, she
actually stood up on her own.

00:11:58.550 --> 00:11:59.630 align:middle line:90%
Amazing.

00:11:59.630 --> 00:12:02.590 align:middle line:90%
Now this is a month after that.

00:12:02.590 --> 00:12:04.810 align:middle line:84%
She is walking
without the walker.

00:12:04.810 --> 00:12:07.290 align:middle line:84%
She can't do it all on her
own yet, because remember,

00:12:07.290 --> 00:12:09.710 align:middle line:84%
it's only been about
seven or eight months

00:12:09.710 --> 00:12:13.710 align:middle line:84%
since her body is able to deal
with this myelin sheath problem.

00:12:13.710 --> 00:12:16.710 align:middle line:90%
But way better.

00:12:16.710 --> 00:12:19.230 align:middle line:84%
Now this is about
nine months later.

00:12:19.230 --> 00:12:22.670 align:middle line:84%
She's on a bike, which
is extraordinary.

00:12:22.670 --> 00:12:25.870 align:middle line:84%
And she's motioning to her
dad to give her a push,

00:12:25.870 --> 00:12:27.510 align:middle line:90%
and to help her out.

00:12:27.510 --> 00:12:31.210 align:middle line:84%
And here she is just a
few months ago this year.

00:12:31.210 --> 00:12:34.350 align:middle line:84%
So this is now about a
year and three months

00:12:34.350 --> 00:12:37.370 align:middle line:90%
after that dose of gene therapy.

00:12:37.370 --> 00:12:40.330 align:middle line:84%
She's about to be able
to walk on her own

00:12:40.330 --> 00:12:43.210 align:middle line:90%
for the very first time.

00:12:43.210 --> 00:12:44.890 align:middle line:84%
There has been no
Canavan patient

00:12:44.890 --> 00:12:46.610 align:middle line:84%
in the history of
the disease that

00:12:46.610 --> 00:12:49.410 align:middle line:90%
has ever been able to do this.

00:12:49.410 --> 00:12:54.010 align:middle line:84%
And so this is remarkable,
remarkable progress.

00:12:54.010 --> 00:12:56.898 align:middle line:90%
- Can you blow me a kiss?

00:12:56.898 --> 00:12:57.710 align:middle line:90%
[KISS SOUND]

00:12:57.710 --> 00:12:58.210 align:middle line:90%


00:12:58.210 --> 00:13:00.330 align:middle line:90%
Thank you, honey.

00:13:00.330 --> 00:13:02.710 align:middle line:90%
- She is actually reading now.

00:13:02.710 --> 00:13:04.910 align:middle line:84%
She's starting to learn the
letters of the alphabet,

00:13:04.910 --> 00:13:06.510 align:middle line:90%
and she's responsive.

00:13:06.510 --> 00:13:07.990 align:middle line:90%
She can actually interact.

00:13:07.990 --> 00:13:09.550 align:middle line:90%
This is extraordinary.

00:13:09.550 --> 00:13:11.110 align:middle line:90%
[END PLAYBACK]

00:13:11.110 --> 00:13:11.610 align:middle line:90%


00:13:11.610 --> 00:13:13.450 align:middle line:84%
ANDREW W. LO: The
blind shall see.

00:13:13.450 --> 00:13:15.262 align:middle line:90%
The lame shall walk.

00:13:15.262 --> 00:13:16.970 align:middle line:84%
That's a phrase that
we usually associate

00:13:16.970 --> 00:13:19.450 align:middle line:90%
with religious experiences.

00:13:19.450 --> 00:13:20.470 align:middle line:90%
But it's happening.

00:13:20.470 --> 00:13:23.450 align:middle line:84%
It's happening now thanks
to the miracles that

00:13:23.450 --> 00:13:27.250 align:middle line:84%
are being developed by these
scientists and clinicians.

00:13:27.250 --> 00:13:31.590 align:middle line:84%
And our colleagues, Phil
Sharpe, Susan Hockfield,

00:13:31.590 --> 00:13:36.240 align:middle line:84%
and Tyler Jacks, they
published a paper in 2016

00:13:36.240 --> 00:13:39.100 align:middle line:84%
to describe this new world
that we're living in.

00:13:39.100 --> 00:13:40.560 align:middle line:90%
They call it, convergence.

00:13:40.560 --> 00:13:43.080 align:middle line:84%
The convergence of the life
sciences, physical sciences,

00:13:43.080 --> 00:13:45.480 align:middle line:84%
and engineering,
all of these fields

00:13:45.480 --> 00:13:48.760 align:middle line:84%
are coming together to be
able to do things that people

00:13:48.760 --> 00:13:51.120 align:middle line:90%
were not able to do before.

00:13:51.120 --> 00:13:53.600 align:middle line:84%
The other term that
people in biomedicine use

00:13:53.600 --> 00:13:56.640 align:middle line:90%
is the omics revolution.

00:13:56.640 --> 00:14:00.640 align:middle line:84%
All of these omics have
experienced tremendous progress

00:14:00.640 --> 00:14:04.320 align:middle line:84%
over the last even five
years, never mind 10 or 20.

00:14:04.320 --> 00:14:08.440 align:middle line:84%
And genomics, the science
of sequencing your genes,

00:14:08.440 --> 00:14:11.480 align:middle line:84%
epigenomics, the on-off switches
that cause certain genes to be

00:14:11.480 --> 00:14:15.800 align:middle line:84%
switched on, and others to be
switched off, transcriptomics,

00:14:15.800 --> 00:14:19.760 align:middle line:84%
the various different ways that
you can turn sequences of genes

00:14:19.760 --> 00:14:22.060 align:middle line:84%
into proteins that
are in your body,

00:14:22.060 --> 00:14:25.720 align:middle line:84%
proteomics the 20,000 to 25,000
proteins that are being produced

00:14:25.720 --> 00:14:27.720 align:middle line:90%
every day by your body.

00:14:27.720 --> 00:14:29.760 align:middle line:84%
Metabolomics, the
chemical reactions

00:14:29.760 --> 00:14:31.760 align:middle line:90%
that make life possible.

00:14:31.760 --> 00:14:35.510 align:middle line:84%
And finally, most recently,
microbiomics, the colonies

00:14:35.510 --> 00:14:39.310 align:middle line:84%
of bacteria that inhabit our
bodies that keep us healthy.

00:14:39.310 --> 00:14:44.590 align:middle line:84%
All of these omics have given
us tremendous new knowledge

00:14:44.590 --> 00:14:47.910 align:middle line:84%
based upon research that's been
going on thanks to convergence,

00:14:47.910 --> 00:14:49.450 align:middle line:90%
with the exception of one.

00:14:49.450 --> 00:14:53.030 align:middle line:84%
There is one omics that's been
the bottleneck to progress.

00:14:53.030 --> 00:14:55.350 align:middle line:90%
And you know what that omics is?

00:14:55.350 --> 00:14:56.750 align:middle line:90%
Economics.

00:14:56.750 --> 00:14:58.370 align:middle line:84%
Because you got
to pay for stuff.

00:14:58.370 --> 00:15:01.230 align:middle line:84%
And we're using the same old
stuff, the same old business

00:15:01.230 --> 00:15:04.950 align:middle line:84%
models, to try to do
those kinds of financings.

00:15:04.950 --> 00:15:08.350 align:middle line:84%
So what I want to tell
you about is a challenge

00:15:08.350 --> 00:15:10.950 align:middle line:84%
that economics is
facing, and how we

00:15:10.950 --> 00:15:12.670 align:middle line:90%
can get around that challenge.

00:15:12.670 --> 00:15:15.878 align:middle line:84%
And the challenge is
this, the valley of death.

00:15:15.878 --> 00:15:18.170 align:middle line:84%
When I first started getting
interested in health care,

00:15:18.170 --> 00:15:23.350 align:middle line:84%
I would talk to scientists, and
doctors in Boston, Cambridge.

00:15:23.350 --> 00:15:27.390 align:middle line:84%
We have a lot of them, as you
know, very, very good ones.

00:15:27.390 --> 00:15:31.393 align:middle line:84%
And I would ask them, why
isn't there more progress?

00:15:31.393 --> 00:15:33.060 align:middle line:84%
And it keeps telling
me about the valley

00:15:33.060 --> 00:15:34.780 align:middle line:90%
of death, the valley of death.

00:15:34.780 --> 00:15:37.300 align:middle line:84%
So I'm thinking, what
does southern California

00:15:37.300 --> 00:15:40.580 align:middle line:84%
have to do with
progress in biomedicine?

00:15:40.580 --> 00:15:42.240 align:middle line:84%
And then they tell
me, no, no, no.

00:15:42.240 --> 00:15:42.980 align:middle line:90%
It's not a place.

00:15:42.980 --> 00:15:45.580 align:middle line:84%
It's actually a
metaphysical concept

00:15:45.580 --> 00:15:50.660 align:middle line:84%
of the period between
scientific experiments

00:15:50.660 --> 00:15:53.060 align:middle line:84%
at university and
medical centers,

00:15:53.060 --> 00:15:56.460 align:middle line:84%
and first in human
clinical trials.

00:15:56.460 --> 00:15:59.780 align:middle line:84%
That period in between
those two milestones

00:15:59.780 --> 00:16:02.300 align:middle line:84%
is the valley of death,
meaning that it's

00:16:02.300 --> 00:16:06.420 align:middle line:84%
really hard to raise money to
fund those kinds of things.

00:16:06.420 --> 00:16:08.140 align:middle line:84%
And so money is
something that I've

00:16:08.140 --> 00:16:09.600 align:middle line:90%
spent a lot of time studying.

00:16:09.600 --> 00:16:11.900 align:middle line:90%
So my initial reaction was, why?

00:16:11.900 --> 00:16:14.640 align:middle line:84%
Why is it the case that
there is a valley of death?

00:16:14.640 --> 00:16:17.060 align:middle line:84%
I just assumed that if
there was a patient in need,

00:16:17.060 --> 00:16:19.160 align:middle line:84%
and there was some technology
that could help that patient,

00:16:19.160 --> 00:16:20.577 align:middle line:84%
that magically,
money would appear

00:16:20.577 --> 00:16:23.140 align:middle line:84%
to fund the drug or a
device, and everything

00:16:23.140 --> 00:16:25.220 align:middle line:90%
would be hunky dory.

00:16:25.220 --> 00:16:28.260 align:middle line:84%
Turns out that that's not
really how the world works.

00:16:28.260 --> 00:16:30.450 align:middle line:84%
And the more I studied
the problem, the more I

00:16:30.450 --> 00:16:33.850 align:middle line:84%
realized that actually,
the valley of death

00:16:33.850 --> 00:16:36.650 align:middle line:84%
exists not just in health
care, but in many fields

00:16:36.650 --> 00:16:39.770 align:middle line:84%
because of increasing
risk and uncertainty.

00:16:39.770 --> 00:16:41.773 align:middle line:84%
Now what do I mean by
risk and uncertainty?

00:16:41.773 --> 00:16:43.190 align:middle line:84%
I think you all
know what risk is.

00:16:43.190 --> 00:16:47.330 align:middle line:84%
Risk is the kind of randomness
that we can parameterize.

00:16:47.330 --> 00:16:51.210 align:middle line:84%
So normal distribution, gamma
distribution, chi squared.

00:16:51.210 --> 00:16:54.290 align:middle line:84%
We can figure out
the probability laws

00:16:54.290 --> 00:16:56.890 align:middle line:90%
of risky things.

00:16:56.890 --> 00:16:59.450 align:middle line:84%
But the uncertain
things, those are

00:16:59.450 --> 00:17:03.570 align:middle line:84%
what economists refer to
as the unknown unknowns,

00:17:03.570 --> 00:17:06.270 align:middle line:84%
because nobody's ever
done them before.

00:17:06.270 --> 00:17:07.310 align:middle line:90%
There's no data.

00:17:07.310 --> 00:17:09.490 align:middle line:84%
So you can't assess
the probability

00:17:09.490 --> 00:17:11.970 align:middle line:90%
that this could possibly happen.

00:17:11.970 --> 00:17:16.650 align:middle line:84%
And so that's the challenge
that the valley of death

00:17:16.650 --> 00:17:19.089 align:middle line:90%
ultimately needs to overcome.

00:17:19.089 --> 00:17:22.810 align:middle line:84%
Because drug development
is a very lengthy process,

00:17:22.810 --> 00:17:24.730 align:middle line:84%
as some of you
may know, you have

00:17:24.730 --> 00:17:26.520 align:middle line:90%
to go through clinical testing.

00:17:26.520 --> 00:17:30.560 align:middle line:84%
And because of that process,
there are three things about

00:17:30.560 --> 00:17:32.920 align:middle line:84%
investing in drug
development that investors

00:17:32.920 --> 00:17:34.380 align:middle line:90%
have to get their arms around.

00:17:34.380 --> 00:17:37.440 align:middle line:84%
One, it costs a
large amount of money

00:17:37.440 --> 00:17:41.840 align:middle line:84%
to do the testing, hundreds of
millions to billions of dollars,

00:17:41.840 --> 00:17:44.920 align:middle line:84%
in order to get
one drug approved.

00:17:44.920 --> 00:17:50.040 align:middle line:84%
Second, that process is
generally very, very unlikely

00:17:50.040 --> 00:17:50.860 align:middle line:90%
to succeed.

00:17:50.860 --> 00:17:53.920 align:middle line:90%
Low probabilities of success.

00:17:53.920 --> 00:17:57.160 align:middle line:84%
And third, it turns out
that developing a drug

00:17:57.160 --> 00:18:00.920 align:middle line:84%
can take 10 to 15 years
from beginning to end.

00:18:00.920 --> 00:18:05.040 align:middle line:84%
So if you compare that
with developing an app,

00:18:05.040 --> 00:18:08.840 align:middle line:84%
how many of you have actually
developed an app already?

00:18:08.840 --> 00:18:12.360 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, so I imagine that it
doesn't take you $100 million

00:18:12.360 --> 00:18:13.500 align:middle line:90%
to do that.

00:18:13.500 --> 00:18:15.500 align:middle line:84%
And it doesn't take
you 10 or 15 years.

00:18:15.500 --> 00:18:17.640 align:middle line:84%
And it doesn't take
you a whole team

00:18:17.640 --> 00:18:19.880 align:middle line:84%
of people, where
you're going to fail

00:18:19.880 --> 00:18:24.080 align:middle line:84%
with a probability
of whatever, 95%.

00:18:24.080 --> 00:18:28.190 align:middle line:84%
So that's the problem
with investing in this.

00:18:28.190 --> 00:18:31.270 align:middle line:84%
And so I'm going to give
you an example of why

00:18:31.270 --> 00:18:36.270 align:middle line:84%
this is so challenging by asking
you to think about investing.

00:18:36.270 --> 00:18:40.190 align:middle line:84%
Some of you may already have
invested, especially given

00:18:40.190 --> 00:18:41.630 align:middle line:90%
that we're at MIT.

00:18:41.630 --> 00:18:43.670 align:middle line:84%
I often encounter
undergraduates who

00:18:43.670 --> 00:18:48.150 align:middle line:84%
are actually quite accomplished
traders and portfolio managers.

00:18:48.150 --> 00:18:50.910 align:middle line:84%
But let me give you
the example that I

00:18:50.910 --> 00:18:54.430 align:middle line:84%
give my first-year MBA students,
and see how you do with this.

00:18:54.430 --> 00:18:56.807 align:middle line:84%
I show them four different
financial investments.

00:18:56.807 --> 00:18:59.390 align:middle line:84%
I don't tell them what they are,
or even over what time period

00:18:59.390 --> 00:19:00.087 align:middle line:90%
they span.

00:19:00.087 --> 00:19:01.670 align:middle line:84%
I just tell them,
this is what happens

00:19:01.670 --> 00:19:03.878 align:middle line:84%
when you put a dollar into
it, and you leave it there

00:19:03.878 --> 00:19:07.390 align:middle line:84%
for an unspecified,
multi-year investment horizon.

00:19:07.390 --> 00:19:10.470 align:middle line:84%
The green line turns
a dollar into $2.00.

00:19:10.470 --> 00:19:13.990 align:middle line:84%
Not very rewarding, but
not particularly risky.

00:19:13.990 --> 00:19:17.950 align:middle line:84%
The red line turns $1.00 into
$5.00, way more rewarding,

00:19:17.950 --> 00:19:21.070 align:middle line:84%
but way more volatile,
lots of ups and downs.

00:19:21.070 --> 00:19:24.100 align:middle line:84%
The blue line turns a
dollar into about $8.00,

00:19:24.100 --> 00:19:28.780 align:middle line:84%
the most rewarding of all,
but also the most volatile.

00:19:28.780 --> 00:19:31.160 align:middle line:84%
And the black line,
somewhere in the middle.

00:19:31.160 --> 00:19:34.420 align:middle line:84%
Not the most rewarding,
but relatively reasonable

00:19:34.420 --> 00:19:35.660 align:middle line:90%
amount of risk.

00:19:35.660 --> 00:19:37.992 align:middle line:84%
So if you could have only
one of these investments,

00:19:37.992 --> 00:19:39.700 align:middle line:84%
you can't mix and
match them, if you only

00:19:39.700 --> 00:19:42.180 align:middle line:84%
can have one to put
your life savings,

00:19:42.180 --> 00:19:47.140 align:middle line:84%
or better yet, to invest your
grandparents' retirement money,

00:19:47.140 --> 00:19:50.660 align:middle line:84%
and you had to do it for them,
they asked you to help them,

00:19:50.660 --> 00:19:52.420 align:middle line:90%
which would you pick?

00:19:52.420 --> 00:19:56.090 align:middle line:84%
How many of you would pick
the green line to invest?

00:19:56.090 --> 00:19:59.540 align:middle line:90%


00:19:59.540 --> 00:20:02.100 align:middle line:90%
Wow, no takers?

00:20:02.100 --> 00:20:03.280 align:middle line:90%
OK, how about the red line?

00:20:03.280 --> 00:20:05.940 align:middle line:84%
How many people would
take the red line?

00:20:05.940 --> 00:20:08.500 align:middle line:90%
No takers for the red line?

00:20:08.500 --> 00:20:10.360 align:middle line:84%
I want you to
remember this moment,

00:20:10.360 --> 00:20:11.860 align:middle line:84%
because I think
some of you may want

00:20:11.860 --> 00:20:13.818 align:middle line:84%
to call your brokers
afterwards when I tell you

00:20:13.818 --> 00:20:15.160 align:middle line:90%
what the red line is.

00:20:15.160 --> 00:20:16.160 align:middle line:90%
How about the blue line?

00:20:16.160 --> 00:20:18.340 align:middle line:84%
How many people want
to do the blue line?

00:20:18.340 --> 00:20:21.410 align:middle line:84%
OK, we got one or two
entrepreneurs in here,

00:20:21.410 --> 00:20:22.790 align:middle line:90%
or hedge fund manager.

00:20:22.790 --> 00:20:23.610 align:middle line:90%
OK good.

00:20:23.610 --> 00:20:24.910 align:middle line:90%
And then now the black line.

00:20:24.910 --> 00:20:25.952 align:middle line:90%
How about the black line?

00:20:25.952 --> 00:20:29.093 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, by far the most popular
choice in my MBA class as well.

00:20:29.093 --> 00:20:30.510 align:middle line:84%
And when I ask
them why, they say,

00:20:30.510 --> 00:20:33.430 align:middle line:84%
well, it's got the best trade
off between risk and reward.

00:20:33.430 --> 00:20:34.650 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, question.

00:20:34.650 --> 00:20:37.330 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Isn't the black
line [INAUDIBLE] Ponzi scheme?

00:20:37.330 --> 00:20:39.130 align:middle line:90%
ANDREW W. LO: Really?

00:20:39.130 --> 00:20:40.090 align:middle line:90%
I don't know.

00:20:40.090 --> 00:20:40.838 align:middle line:90%
Let's see.

00:20:40.838 --> 00:20:43.130 align:middle line:84%
First of all, let me tell
you what the four assets are.

00:20:43.130 --> 00:20:45.170 align:middle line:90%
And we'll talk about that.

00:20:45.170 --> 00:20:48.970 align:middle line:84%
The four assets are the
green line, treasury bills,

00:20:48.970 --> 00:20:52.190 align:middle line:84%
safest asset in the world, at
least for the next few weeks.

00:20:52.190 --> 00:20:55.330 align:middle line:84%
We'll see whether the
budget discussions go well.

00:20:55.330 --> 00:20:58.720 align:middle line:84%
As long as we agree to pay back
our debts, yes, safe asset.

00:20:58.720 --> 00:20:59.470 align:middle line:90%
But you know what?

00:20:59.470 --> 00:21:01.070 align:middle line:84%
You put your money
in it in 2008,

00:21:01.070 --> 00:21:02.952 align:middle line:84%
you would have earned
pretty much nothing.

00:21:02.952 --> 00:21:04.410 align:middle line:84%
And actually, you
would have earned

00:21:04.410 --> 00:21:06.630 align:middle line:84%
less than nothing
because of inflation

00:21:06.630 --> 00:21:09.210 align:middle line:90%
if you look at the real return.

00:21:09.210 --> 00:21:09.710 align:middle line:90%
All right.

00:21:09.710 --> 00:21:12.130 align:middle line:84%
The red line that
none of you picked,

00:21:12.130 --> 00:21:14.030 align:middle line:90%
that is the US stock market.

00:21:14.030 --> 00:21:16.330 align:middle line:84%
I bet you some of
you may already have

00:21:16.330 --> 00:21:18.670 align:middle line:90%
an investment in the S&P 500.

00:21:18.670 --> 00:21:19.416 align:middle line:90%
Right?

00:21:19.416 --> 00:21:22.610 align:middle line:84%
But if you didn't pick it, or
if you did pick it in 2008,

00:21:22.610 --> 00:21:23.890 align:middle line:90%
congratulations.

00:21:23.890 --> 00:21:26.590 align:middle line:84%
You would have done
just fine since then.

00:21:26.590 --> 00:21:29.390 align:middle line:90%
Very, very good return.

00:21:29.390 --> 00:21:32.250 align:middle line:84%
The blue line that only
one of two of you picked,

00:21:32.250 --> 00:21:35.370 align:middle line:84%
that is the single
pharmaceutical company, Pfizer,

00:21:35.370 --> 00:21:37.710 align:middle line:84%
one of the largest pharma
companies in the world.

00:21:37.710 --> 00:21:40.010 align:middle line:84%
And if you had picked
Pfizer in 2008,

00:21:40.010 --> 00:21:41.890 align:middle line:90%
well, double congratulations.

00:21:41.890 --> 00:21:45.290 align:middle line:84%
You would have done
spectacularly well since then.

00:21:45.290 --> 00:21:49.230 align:middle line:84%
Lots of volatility,
but really quite well.

00:21:49.230 --> 00:21:52.670 align:middle line:84%
Now the black line, that
is the returns to a fund

00:21:52.670 --> 00:21:56.230 align:middle line:84%
called the Fairfield Sentry
Fund, which most of you

00:21:56.230 --> 00:21:57.570 align:middle line:90%
have probably never heard of.

00:21:57.570 --> 00:22:01.750 align:middle line:84%
But that was the feeder fund for
the Bernie Madoff Ponzi scheme.

00:22:01.750 --> 00:22:03.130 align:middle line:90%
So quite right.

00:22:03.130 --> 00:22:07.270 align:middle line:90%
You got the correct answer.

00:22:07.270 --> 00:22:10.150 align:middle line:84%
I had to stop it in 2008 because
the Ponzi scheme ended up

00:22:10.150 --> 00:22:12.190 align:middle line:90%
blowing up then.

00:22:12.190 --> 00:22:14.870 align:middle line:84%
Now I show this
example to students

00:22:14.870 --> 00:22:17.190 align:middle line:84%
because I want to
explain to them something

00:22:17.190 --> 00:22:19.200 align:middle line:90%
very common about human nature.

00:22:19.200 --> 00:22:21.620 align:middle line:84%
And that is that we
all, as investors, we

00:22:21.620 --> 00:22:24.360 align:middle line:84%
want high yielding,
low-risk assets.

00:22:24.360 --> 00:22:27.280 align:middle line:84%
And we have a name in
finance that describes it.

00:22:27.280 --> 00:22:29.840 align:middle line:84%
It's called the Sharpe
ratio, after Bill Sharpe,

00:22:29.840 --> 00:22:32.500 align:middle line:84%
the Nobel-Prize-winning
economist from Stanford,

00:22:32.500 --> 00:22:36.180 align:middle line:84%
who pointed out that people
care about the expected return

00:22:36.180 --> 00:22:40.380 align:middle line:84%
of an investment above
T-bills per unit of risk

00:22:40.380 --> 00:22:41.360 align:middle line:90%
that they're taking.

00:22:41.360 --> 00:22:46.020 align:middle line:84%
They want to have the highest
expected return per unit risk.

00:22:46.020 --> 00:22:47.620 align:middle line:90%
High Sharpe ratios.

00:22:47.620 --> 00:22:50.320 align:middle line:84%
So what's the Sharpe ratio
of these investments?

00:22:50.320 --> 00:22:54.108 align:middle line:84%
Well, it's 0.33 for
the S&P, point--

00:22:54.108 --> 00:23:00.500 align:middle line:84%
sorry, 0.34 for Pfizer, 0.33
for the S&P. And on paper,

00:23:00.500 --> 00:23:04.020 align:middle line:84%
the Ponzi scheme had a Sharpe
ratio an order of magnitude

00:23:04.020 --> 00:23:05.260 align:middle line:90%
higher.

00:23:05.260 --> 00:23:07.580 align:middle line:84%
And that's how Bernie
Madoff suckered

00:23:07.580 --> 00:23:13.060 align:middle line:84%
all of these poor investors to
put money with him. $50 billion

00:23:13.060 --> 00:23:16.650 align:middle line:84%
of investor money that
he essentially stole

00:23:16.650 --> 00:23:19.650 align:middle line:90%
because of this common fact.

00:23:19.650 --> 00:23:23.930 align:middle line:84%
Now it turns out
that biomedicine

00:23:23.930 --> 00:23:26.850 align:middle line:84%
has had a Sharpe ratio
that's been declining

00:23:26.850 --> 00:23:30.850 align:middle line:84%
over the last several decades
not because the numerator is

00:23:30.850 --> 00:23:31.550 align:middle line:90%
unattractive.

00:23:31.550 --> 00:23:35.570 align:middle line:84%
There are many biotech companies
that just do super well.

00:23:35.570 --> 00:23:37.550 align:middle line:84%
But the problem
is the numerator.

00:23:37.550 --> 00:23:40.370 align:middle line:84%
The numerator is
growing even faster.

00:23:40.370 --> 00:23:44.490 align:middle line:84%
The risk, as well
as the uncertainty

00:23:44.490 --> 00:23:50.850 align:middle line:84%
is growing as we become more
sophisticated about the science

00:23:50.850 --> 00:23:54.130 align:middle line:90%
and medicine of disease.

00:23:54.130 --> 00:23:58.210 align:middle line:84%
And so we need to figure out how
to increase the Sharpe ratio.

00:23:58.210 --> 00:24:00.690 align:middle line:90%
That's the goal.

00:24:00.690 --> 00:24:02.190 align:middle line:90%
So I know this is a math class.

00:24:02.190 --> 00:24:05.370 align:middle line:84%
So I could not possibly
do a presentation here

00:24:05.370 --> 00:24:07.690 align:middle line:84%
without giving you at
least one equation.

00:24:07.690 --> 00:24:09.530 align:middle line:84%
So I'm going to give
you an equation.

00:24:09.530 --> 00:24:11.290 align:middle line:84%
And it's going to be
a very difficult math

00:24:11.290 --> 00:24:16.800 align:middle line:84%
challenge about how you can
increase the Sharpe ratio.

00:24:16.800 --> 00:24:20.920 align:middle line:84%
It turns out there are two ways
of increasing the Sharpe ratio.

00:24:20.920 --> 00:24:22.920 align:middle line:84%
And let me tell you,
for those of you who

00:24:22.920 --> 00:24:26.120 align:middle line:84%
aren't as mathematically
gifted as I am,

00:24:26.120 --> 00:24:29.040 align:middle line:84%
you can increase the
numerator, or you

00:24:29.040 --> 00:24:30.880 align:middle line:90%
can decrease the denominator.

00:24:30.880 --> 00:24:32.760 align:middle line:90%
Those are the two ways.

00:24:32.760 --> 00:24:34.453 align:middle line:84%
Now of course, in
my MBA class, I

00:24:34.453 --> 00:24:36.620 align:middle line:84%
have to have a student who
raises his hand and says,

00:24:36.620 --> 00:24:38.400 align:middle line:84%
Professor Lo, there's
actually a third way.

00:24:38.400 --> 00:24:39.358 align:middle line:90%
And I said, oh, really?

00:24:39.358 --> 00:24:40.840 align:middle line:90%
He said, yes, you can do both.

00:24:40.840 --> 00:24:41.860 align:middle line:90%
So yes, I agree.

00:24:41.860 --> 00:24:43.680 align:middle line:90%
You can do both.

00:24:43.680 --> 00:24:46.360 align:middle line:84%
It turns out that
we are doing both.

00:24:46.360 --> 00:24:48.600 align:middle line:84%
Better science,
better engineering,

00:24:48.600 --> 00:24:50.820 align:middle line:84%
better organizational
structures, on and on.

00:24:50.820 --> 00:24:54.080 align:middle line:84%
All of those are designed
to increase the numerator

00:24:54.080 --> 00:24:56.560 align:middle line:90%
and decrease the denominator.

00:24:56.560 --> 00:25:00.040 align:middle line:84%
But the one set of
techniques that have not

00:25:00.040 --> 00:25:03.440 align:middle line:84%
been used as much in biomedicine
as well as in other industries

00:25:03.440 --> 00:25:09.000 align:middle line:84%
that I'm about to tell you
is new financing and business

00:25:09.000 --> 00:25:11.280 align:middle line:90%
models.

00:25:11.280 --> 00:25:14.650 align:middle line:84%
And that's the interesting
opportunity for mathematicians,

00:25:14.650 --> 00:25:15.750 align:middle line:90%
believe it or not.

00:25:15.750 --> 00:25:18.270 align:middle line:84%
There are ways of constructing
new business models

00:25:18.270 --> 00:25:22.630 align:middle line:84%
using math that could actually
change the risk-reward trade

00:25:22.630 --> 00:25:24.938 align:middle line:90%
offs of these investments.

00:25:24.938 --> 00:25:25.730 align:middle line:90%
So let me show you.

00:25:25.730 --> 00:25:27.710 align:middle line:90%
I'll give you an example.

00:25:27.710 --> 00:25:31.110 align:middle line:84%
Suppose I give
you an opportunity

00:25:31.110 --> 00:25:35.190 align:middle line:84%
to invest in a new
fund that I'm creating.

00:25:35.190 --> 00:25:39.630 align:middle line:84%
I need $200 million
to launch my fund.

00:25:39.630 --> 00:25:41.630 align:middle line:84%
And I'm going to be
investing in all sorts

00:25:41.630 --> 00:25:44.810 align:middle line:84%
of special technologies
that only I have access to.

00:25:44.810 --> 00:25:47.990 align:middle line:90%
You don't, So it's proprietary.

00:25:47.990 --> 00:25:50.350 align:middle line:84%
It's going to take me 10
years before I give you

00:25:50.350 --> 00:25:52.310 align:middle line:90%
any payoff whatsoever.

00:25:52.310 --> 00:25:56.790 align:middle line:84%
So don't bother asking me before
the decade is out whether or not

00:25:56.790 --> 00:25:58.710 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to give
you anything back.

00:25:58.710 --> 00:26:01.470 align:middle line:84%
It's going to take
at least that long.

00:26:01.470 --> 00:26:03.910 align:middle line:84%
And in full disclosure,
the likelihood

00:26:03.910 --> 00:26:08.270 align:middle line:84%
that I give you
anything back is 5%.

00:26:08.270 --> 00:26:13.460 align:middle line:84%
95% of the time, I'm not going
to be able to produce anything

00:26:13.460 --> 00:26:14.540 align:middle line:90%
after 10 years.

00:26:14.540 --> 00:26:15.780 align:middle line:90%
OK?

00:26:15.780 --> 00:26:19.420 align:middle line:84%
How many of you
would invest with me?

00:26:19.420 --> 00:26:21.260 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Depends on the return.

00:26:21.260 --> 00:26:22.540 align:middle line:90%
ANDREW W. LO: Thank you.

00:26:22.540 --> 00:26:24.800 align:middle line:84%
Most of you didn't even
bother raising your hand.

00:26:24.800 --> 00:26:26.740 align:middle line:84%
But you, you want to
know what the return is.

00:26:26.740 --> 00:26:27.480 align:middle line:90%
Fair enough.

00:26:27.480 --> 00:26:29.300 align:middle line:90%
That's absolutely right.

00:26:29.300 --> 00:26:32.940 align:middle line:84%
For most of my MBA students,
they don't bother asking.

00:26:32.940 --> 00:26:40.860 align:middle line:84%
When they hear $200 million,
wait 10 years, 95% failure rate?

00:26:40.860 --> 00:26:41.640 align:middle line:90%
No thank you.

00:26:41.640 --> 00:26:43.380 align:middle line:90%
Not interested.

00:26:43.380 --> 00:26:45.980 align:middle line:84%
But I will get one or
two MBA students who

00:26:45.980 --> 00:26:48.220 align:middle line:90%
will ask, what's the return?

00:26:48.220 --> 00:26:50.163 align:middle line:90%
So let me tell you.

00:26:50.163 --> 00:26:51.580 align:middle line:84%
These are the
back-of-the-envelope

00:26:51.580 --> 00:26:57.340 align:middle line:84%
calculations for what a single
anti-cancer drug will do

00:26:57.340 --> 00:27:02.220 align:middle line:84%
in terms of potential
revenues and success rates.

00:27:02.220 --> 00:27:06.420 align:middle line:84%
It takes about $200 million
to do the clinical trials.

00:27:06.420 --> 00:27:08.690 align:middle line:84%
It takes about 10
years to do them.

00:27:08.690 --> 00:27:11.370 align:middle line:84%
And in oncology, the
historical success rate

00:27:11.370 --> 00:27:13.910 align:middle line:84%
is actually a
little less than 5%.

00:27:13.910 --> 00:27:15.190 align:middle line:90%
3.4%.

00:27:15.190 --> 00:27:18.530 align:middle line:84%
But I'm going to use
5% as round numbers.

00:27:18.530 --> 00:27:22.610 align:middle line:84%
If you are successful, though,
in the case of cancer drugs,

00:27:22.610 --> 00:27:29.170 align:middle line:84%
you will get $2 billion of
revenues from years 11 to 20.

00:27:29.170 --> 00:27:30.350 align:middle line:90%
Why 11 to 20?

00:27:30.350 --> 00:27:34.130 align:middle line:84%
Well, because it takes 10
years to develop a cancer drug.

00:27:34.130 --> 00:27:36.410 align:middle line:84%
So if you're successful,
then starting in year 11

00:27:36.410 --> 00:27:39.130 align:middle line:84%
is when you begin to
generate cash flows.

00:27:39.130 --> 00:27:41.570 align:middle line:90%
And why until year 20?

00:27:41.570 --> 00:27:44.930 align:middle line:84%
It's because that's how
long a patent lasts.

00:27:44.930 --> 00:27:48.130 align:middle line:84%
After year 20, your
patent is gone.

00:27:48.130 --> 00:27:53.410 align:middle line:84%
And people can compete with you,
and your profits go way down.

00:27:53.410 --> 00:27:56.670 align:middle line:84%
Two billion, two billion, two
billion from years 11 to 20.

00:27:56.670 --> 00:28:02.890 align:middle line:84%
That is equivalent to a
single payday of $12.3 billion

00:28:02.890 --> 00:28:05.090 align:middle line:90%
in year 10.

00:28:05.090 --> 00:28:08.360 align:middle line:84%
OK, so now let me ask you
the question one more time.

00:28:08.360 --> 00:28:10.880 align:middle line:90%
I need $200 million up front.

00:28:10.880 --> 00:28:12.640 align:middle line:90%
Wait 10 years.

00:28:12.640 --> 00:28:17.680 align:middle line:84%
95% of the time, I'll give you
nothing, maybe a warm handshake

00:28:17.680 --> 00:28:19.160 align:middle line:90%
and goodbye.

00:28:19.160 --> 00:28:23.720 align:middle line:84%
And 5% of the time, I'll give
you a check for $12.3 billion

00:28:23.720 --> 00:28:25.160 align:middle line:90%
in year 10.

00:28:25.160 --> 00:28:27.550 align:middle line:84%
How many of you
would invest in that?

00:28:27.550 --> 00:28:30.320 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: It would
depend on [INAUDIBLE].

00:28:30.320 --> 00:28:32.120 align:middle line:90%
ANDREW W. LO: No.

00:28:32.120 --> 00:28:33.080 align:middle line:90%
No.

00:28:33.080 --> 00:28:33.920 align:middle line:90%
I'll take whatever--

00:28:33.920 --> 00:28:35.840 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:28:35.840 --> 00:28:37.480 align:middle line:84%
ANDREW W. LO: Whatever
money you have.

00:28:37.480 --> 00:28:38.680 align:middle line:90%
How many of you?

00:28:38.680 --> 00:28:39.180 align:middle line:90%
All right.

00:28:39.180 --> 00:28:42.360 align:middle line:84%
I'm getting one or two
hands, but not a lot.

00:28:42.360 --> 00:28:45.220 align:middle line:84%
You're not all interested,
because for most of you,

00:28:45.220 --> 00:28:46.640 align:middle line:90%
it was way too risky.

00:28:46.640 --> 00:28:50.160 align:middle line:84%
It takes too long, probability
of success is too low,

00:28:50.160 --> 00:28:52.640 align:middle line:84%
and it's just not
your cup of tea.

00:28:52.640 --> 00:28:55.920 align:middle line:84%
And sure enough, if we
calculate the rate of return,

00:28:55.920 --> 00:28:58.680 align:middle line:84%
this is an expected rate of
return over this 10-year period

00:28:58.680 --> 00:29:05.340 align:middle line:84%
of about 12% with the standard
deviation of 423.5% for a Sharpe

00:29:05.340 --> 00:29:09.580 align:middle line:90%
ratio of close to 0.

00:29:09.580 --> 00:29:14.620 align:middle line:84%
Nobody's interested in
this, or very few people.

00:29:14.620 --> 00:29:16.980 align:middle line:84%
So this is where
financial engineering

00:29:16.980 --> 00:29:19.760 align:middle line:84%
can make a difference, and
where math comes into play.

00:29:19.760 --> 00:29:23.220 align:middle line:84%
A very, very small
amount of math.

00:29:23.220 --> 00:29:26.240 align:middle line:84%
Instead of investing
in one of these drugs,

00:29:26.240 --> 00:29:29.180 align:middle line:84%
why not invest in, oh, I
don't know, 150 of them

00:29:29.180 --> 00:29:31.500 align:middle line:90%
all at the same time?

00:29:31.500 --> 00:29:34.167 align:middle line:84%
If we did that, if we
invested 150, you might say,

00:29:34.167 --> 00:29:35.000 align:middle line:90%
well, wait a minute.

00:29:35.000 --> 00:29:40.640 align:middle line:84%
That takes 150 times 200
million, which is $30 billion.

00:29:40.640 --> 00:29:43.880 align:middle line:84%
That's not the kind of math
that would make anybody excited.

00:29:43.880 --> 00:29:44.720 align:middle line:90%
$30 billion.

00:29:44.720 --> 00:29:47.620 align:middle line:84%
Where are you going
to get $30 billion?

00:29:47.620 --> 00:29:50.140 align:middle line:84%
And as an economist, I have a
very simple answer for that.

00:29:50.140 --> 00:29:53.040 align:middle line:84%
The answer is, assume
we have $30 billion.

00:29:53.040 --> 00:29:56.022 align:middle line:90%


00:29:56.022 --> 00:29:57.480 align:middle line:84%
I'll come back to
this in a minute.

00:29:57.480 --> 00:30:00.420 align:middle line:84%
But if we had $30
billion, and if we

00:30:00.420 --> 00:30:04.370 align:middle line:84%
assume that the 150 projects
are statistically independent

00:30:04.370 --> 00:30:06.370 align:middle line:84%
and identically
distributed, and I'll

00:30:06.370 --> 00:30:10.410 align:middle line:84%
come back to that assumption
later, if that's the case,

00:30:10.410 --> 00:30:14.650 align:middle line:84%
then the expected rate of
return for the portfolio

00:30:14.650 --> 00:30:18.610 align:middle line:84%
is the same because
they're identical projects.

00:30:18.610 --> 00:30:21.930 align:middle line:84%
But the risk now goes
down by the square root

00:30:21.930 --> 00:30:23.310 align:middle line:90%
of the number of projects.

00:30:23.310 --> 00:30:23.810 align:middle line:90%
Why?

00:30:23.810 --> 00:30:26.290 align:middle line:84%
Because the variance
of the sum is

00:30:26.290 --> 00:30:30.130 align:middle line:84%
the sum of the variances
when they're IID.

00:30:30.130 --> 00:30:34.572 align:middle line:84%
And so if the variance is the
sum of the individual variances,

00:30:34.572 --> 00:30:37.030 align:middle line:84%
take the square root of that
to get the standard deviation.

00:30:37.030 --> 00:30:41.390 align:middle line:84%
That's why it goes down by the
rate of the square root of 150,

00:30:41.390 --> 00:30:46.770 align:middle line:84%
which gives you a
34.6% risk rate.

00:30:46.770 --> 00:30:49.970 align:middle line:84%
Now if I calculate the
Sharpe ratio of this,

00:30:49.970 --> 00:30:54.930 align:middle line:84%
I'm getting a Sharpe ratio that
is an order of magnitude higher.

00:30:54.930 --> 00:30:57.950 align:middle line:84%
How many of you would
invest in this fund?

00:30:57.950 --> 00:31:00.090 align:middle line:90%
Show of hands.

00:31:00.090 --> 00:31:03.000 align:middle line:90%
I'm getting a lot more hands.

00:31:03.000 --> 00:31:05.080 align:middle line:84%
That's where I'm going
to get the money.

00:31:05.080 --> 00:31:10.360 align:middle line:84%
I don't have 30 billion,
but you do in the aggregate.

00:31:10.360 --> 00:31:14.600 align:middle line:84%
If I can make this investment
attractive enough to get you

00:31:14.600 --> 00:31:18.040 align:middle line:84%
to put some of
your 401(k) money,

00:31:18.040 --> 00:31:20.680 align:middle line:84%
or some of your summer
internship money,

00:31:20.680 --> 00:31:25.060 align:middle line:84%
into a fund like this, I
can raise $30 billion very,

00:31:25.060 --> 00:31:26.600 align:middle line:90%
very quickly.

00:31:26.600 --> 00:31:30.300 align:middle line:84%
Now the question is, can you
really raise $30 billion?

00:31:30.300 --> 00:31:33.520 align:middle line:84%
And the answer, of
course, is it depends.

00:31:33.520 --> 00:31:36.180 align:middle line:84%
It depends on these
calculations that I just did.

00:31:36.180 --> 00:31:41.040 align:middle line:84%
It depends on the math
underlying this portfolio.

00:31:41.040 --> 00:31:44.680 align:middle line:84%
The mathematics will tell you
whether or not this is feasible.

00:31:44.680 --> 00:31:50.560 align:middle line:84%
So I'll give you an example,
where not only should

00:31:50.560 --> 00:31:52.700 align:middle line:84%
it work, but it
actually does work.

00:31:52.700 --> 00:31:55.720 align:middle line:84%
And the example has to
do with rare diseases.

00:31:55.720 --> 00:31:58.590 align:middle line:84%
So I gave you two
examples of rare diseases

00:31:58.590 --> 00:32:00.670 align:middle line:84%
at the beginning, Canavan
disease, and Leber's

00:32:00.670 --> 00:32:02.110 align:middle line:90%
congenital amaurosis.

00:32:02.110 --> 00:32:10.590 align:middle line:84%
Turns out, there are roughly
12,000 different rare diseases.

00:32:10.590 --> 00:32:13.630 align:middle line:84%
And so individually,
a rare disease

00:32:13.630 --> 00:32:17.090 align:middle line:84%
may have a small patient
population like cystic fibrosis,

00:32:17.090 --> 00:32:20.110 align:middle line:84%
hemophilia, Lou
Gehrig's disease,

00:32:20.110 --> 00:32:23.350 align:middle line:90%
or a number of other diseases.

00:32:23.350 --> 00:32:27.230 align:middle line:84%
But in aggregate, there are more
than 30 million Americans that

00:32:27.230 --> 00:32:31.470 align:middle line:84%
are suffering from one
of these 12,000 diseases.

00:32:31.470 --> 00:32:34.350 align:middle line:84%
I suspect that
somebody in this room

00:32:34.350 --> 00:32:37.230 align:middle line:84%
knows a friend or
a family that's

00:32:37.230 --> 00:32:38.570 align:middle line:90%
suffering from a rare disease.

00:32:38.570 --> 00:32:39.070 align:middle line:90%
Anybody?

00:32:39.070 --> 00:32:40.190 align:middle line:90%
Just show of hands.

00:32:40.190 --> 00:32:40.810 align:middle line:90%
Do you?

00:32:40.810 --> 00:32:41.710 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:32:41.710 --> 00:32:45.710 align:middle line:84%
So it is clear that it may
be rare for any individual,

00:32:45.710 --> 00:32:48.530 align:middle line:84%
but it is not rare
over a population,

00:32:48.530 --> 00:32:52.950 align:middle line:84%
because of just the number of
mutations that are out there.

00:32:52.950 --> 00:32:59.380 align:middle line:84%
And so for a lot of reasons,
it's actually relatively easier

00:32:59.380 --> 00:33:02.020 align:middle line:84%
to develop a drug for
a rare disease, both

00:33:02.020 --> 00:33:04.620 align:middle line:84%
because typically,
we know what's wrong.

00:33:04.620 --> 00:33:06.560 align:middle line:90%
There's a very serious problem.

00:33:06.560 --> 00:33:10.300 align:middle line:84%
And you can trace the origins
to one particular organ,

00:33:10.300 --> 00:33:12.780 align:middle line:84%
and within that organ, to
one particular mechanism,

00:33:12.780 --> 00:33:15.060 align:middle line:84%
and to that mechanism,
one particular gene.

00:33:15.060 --> 00:33:17.060 align:middle line:90%
So you know a lot about it.

00:33:17.060 --> 00:33:23.740 align:middle line:84%
But the other reason it's often
more successful to develop

00:33:23.740 --> 00:33:26.780 align:middle line:84%
these therapeutics is because
it doesn't cost as much money

00:33:26.780 --> 00:33:29.000 align:middle line:90%
because it's a rare population.

00:33:29.000 --> 00:33:30.720 align:middle line:84%
So when you run the
clinical trials,

00:33:30.720 --> 00:33:33.400 align:middle line:84%
you can run them with a
smaller number of people

00:33:33.400 --> 00:33:36.140 align:middle line:84%
and still be able to
demonstrate that the drug works,

00:33:36.140 --> 00:33:38.380 align:middle line:84%
like the case for
Spark Therapeutics.

00:33:38.380 --> 00:33:41.840 align:middle line:84%
In the case of that Leber's
congenital amaurosis,

00:33:41.840 --> 00:33:44.340 align:middle line:84%
I think they got an FDA
approval with something

00:33:44.340 --> 00:33:46.620 align:middle line:90%
like 30 or 40 patients.

00:33:46.620 --> 00:33:49.900 align:middle line:84%
That's it, 30 or 40 patients
in the clinical trial.

00:33:49.900 --> 00:33:52.473 align:middle line:84%
And when they were able
to restore their sight,

00:33:52.473 --> 00:33:54.640 align:middle line:84%
the FDA said, you don't
need to do any more testing.

00:33:54.640 --> 00:33:55.640 align:middle line:90%
We're ready to prove it.

00:33:55.640 --> 00:33:57.810 align:middle line:90%
This is an amazing drug.

00:33:57.810 --> 00:34:00.570 align:middle line:84%
But the key
component is the fact

00:34:00.570 --> 00:34:02.410 align:middle line:84%
that because these
rare diseases are

00:34:02.410 --> 00:34:05.330 align:middle line:84%
so different from
each other, there's

00:34:05.330 --> 00:34:07.890 align:middle line:84%
very little correlation
in the successes

00:34:07.890 --> 00:34:11.530 align:middle line:84%
and failures of different
therapeutic programs.

00:34:11.530 --> 00:34:15.250 align:middle line:84%
So when you calculate
the variance of the sum,

00:34:15.250 --> 00:34:18.170 align:middle line:84%
it really is the sum
of the variances.

00:34:18.170 --> 00:34:20.250 align:middle line:84%
If we don't have
correlation, then

00:34:20.250 --> 00:34:22.090 align:middle line:84%
can anybody tell
me what the formula

00:34:22.090 --> 00:34:25.400 align:middle line:84%
is for the variance of a
sum of random variables?

00:34:25.400 --> 00:34:26.650 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Sum of the variance?

00:34:26.650 --> 00:34:28.067 align:middle line:84%
ANDREW W. LO: Sum
of the variance.

00:34:28.067 --> 00:34:29.150 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: It's covariance.

00:34:29.150 --> 00:34:30.090 align:middle line:90%
ANDREW W. LO: Exactly.

00:34:30.090 --> 00:34:33.242 align:middle line:90%
How many covariances?

00:34:33.242 --> 00:34:35.156 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:34:35.156 --> 00:34:35.822 align:middle line:90%


00:34:35.822 --> 00:34:36.989 align:middle line:90%
ANDREW W. LO: A lot of them.

00:34:36.989 --> 00:34:38.250 align:middle line:90%
Exactly.

00:34:38.250 --> 00:34:41.070 align:middle line:84%
Because you've got A
versus B, A versus C,

00:34:41.070 --> 00:34:45.210 align:middle line:84%
A versus D, B versus C,
B-- all the combinations.

00:34:45.210 --> 00:34:47.690 align:middle line:84%
And so it turns out that
when there's correlation,

00:34:47.690 --> 00:34:49.667 align:middle line:90%
it's almost always positive.

00:34:49.667 --> 00:34:51.250 align:middle line:84%
And so when you
calculate the variance

00:34:51.250 --> 00:34:55.520 align:middle line:84%
of a sum of a portfolio
of typical investments,

00:34:55.520 --> 00:34:57.940 align:middle line:84%
you're getting not just
the individual variances,

00:34:57.940 --> 00:35:00.160 align:middle line:84%
but all the pairwise
covariances.

00:35:00.160 --> 00:35:03.340 align:middle line:84%
If there aren't any
pairwise covariances,

00:35:03.340 --> 00:35:06.440 align:middle line:84%
that's where the risk
reduction comes from.

00:35:06.440 --> 00:35:09.960 align:middle line:84%
And so rare diseases have this
really cool property that I

00:35:09.960 --> 00:35:11.460 align:middle line:90%
have to admit, I'm quite proud.

00:35:11.460 --> 00:35:14.720 align:middle line:84%
I was the one who wrote a
paper that recognized it.

00:35:14.720 --> 00:35:17.200 align:middle line:84%
Frankly, I didn't think
it was that big a deal

00:35:17.200 --> 00:35:19.260 align:middle line:84%
except for this
particular application.

00:35:19.260 --> 00:35:21.700 align:middle line:84%
But it turns out that
in the biomedical field,

00:35:21.700 --> 00:35:23.560 align:middle line:84%
nobody had ever
thought about this.

00:35:23.560 --> 00:35:26.100 align:middle line:84%
The fact that because the
diseases were so different,

00:35:26.100 --> 00:35:30.680 align:middle line:84%
you ought to develop them
not separately, but together

00:35:30.680 --> 00:35:33.840 align:middle line:84%
in a single portfolio to
get the risk reduction.

00:35:33.840 --> 00:35:38.440 align:middle line:84%
So instead of $30 billion, you
only need 400 to 500 million.

00:35:38.440 --> 00:35:41.800 align:middle line:84%
Instead of 150 projects,
you only need 10 or 20

00:35:41.800 --> 00:35:44.200 align:middle line:90%
to get diversification.

00:35:44.200 --> 00:35:47.680 align:middle line:84%
And so what did I do
with this information?

00:35:47.680 --> 00:35:49.040 align:middle line:90%
I wrote a paper.

00:35:49.040 --> 00:35:50.360 align:middle line:90%
That's what we do in academia.

00:35:50.360 --> 00:35:54.230 align:middle line:84%
Published a paper, ran
a Monte Carlo simulation

00:35:54.230 --> 00:36:00.190 align:middle line:84%
to show that if you actually
used relevant industry numbers,

00:36:00.190 --> 00:36:02.630 align:middle line:84%
the kind of rate of
return you're looking at

00:36:02.630 --> 00:36:06.710 align:middle line:90%
is around 22% per year.

00:36:06.710 --> 00:36:09.270 align:middle line:84%
And with the
volatility, that could

00:36:09.270 --> 00:36:11.110 align:middle line:84%
be quite a bit lower
than that, meaning

00:36:11.110 --> 00:36:13.950 align:middle line:90%
a Sharpe ratio greater than 1.

00:36:13.950 --> 00:36:18.230 align:middle line:84%
Now you can ask Jake afterwards
about whether or not a Sharpe

00:36:18.230 --> 00:36:20.430 align:middle line:90%
ratio greater than 1 is good.

00:36:20.430 --> 00:36:24.070 align:middle line:84%
As a professional investor and
a risk manager for A Harvard

00:36:24.070 --> 00:36:26.390 align:middle line:84%
management company, he
can attest to the fact

00:36:26.390 --> 00:36:29.070 align:middle line:84%
that this is a very
attractive and very rare

00:36:29.070 --> 00:36:31.470 align:middle line:90%
kind of opportunity.

00:36:31.470 --> 00:36:34.790 align:middle line:84%
But remember, there are
12,000 rare diseases.

00:36:34.790 --> 00:36:37.430 align:middle line:84%
So it doesn't seem
that rare to me.

00:36:37.430 --> 00:36:41.070 align:middle line:84%
So the question is,
does this really work?

00:36:41.070 --> 00:36:43.990 align:middle line:84%
And the reason I wrote the paper
was because I wanted people

00:36:43.990 --> 00:36:45.130 align:middle line:90%
to try it out.

00:36:45.130 --> 00:36:49.100 align:middle line:84%
And I got a phone call shortly
after the paper came out

00:36:49.100 --> 00:36:50.860 align:middle line:90%
from a former student of mine.

00:36:50.860 --> 00:36:54.540 align:middle line:84%
Somebody who was a PhD in
chemical engineering here at MIT

00:36:54.540 --> 00:36:57.420 align:middle line:84%
took one of my courses when
he was here, then went on,

00:36:57.420 --> 00:37:00.460 align:middle line:84%
and became a biotech VC, and
called me up, said, Professor

00:37:00.460 --> 00:37:01.400 align:middle line:90%
Lo, I read your paper.

00:37:01.400 --> 00:37:02.940 align:middle line:90%
Really interesting.

00:37:02.940 --> 00:37:05.920 align:middle line:84%
Would you mind rerunning
your simulations,

00:37:05.920 --> 00:37:09.380 align:middle line:84%
not with your assumptions,
but with mine?

00:37:09.380 --> 00:37:11.460 align:middle line:84%
And I said to this former
student, of course.

00:37:11.460 --> 00:37:12.240 align:middle line:90%
I'd be happy to.

00:37:12.240 --> 00:37:14.282 align:middle line:84%
You know a lot more about
the industry than I do.

00:37:14.282 --> 00:37:15.760 align:middle line:84%
I'm just using
industry averages,

00:37:15.760 --> 00:37:17.860 align:middle line:84%
whereas you actually
have hard data based

00:37:17.860 --> 00:37:20.220 align:middle line:84%
upon your own
portfolio investments.

00:37:20.220 --> 00:37:24.880 align:middle line:84%
So we ran a model with his
parameters instead of mine.

00:37:24.880 --> 00:37:27.980 align:middle line:84%
And the results,
they look better.

00:37:27.980 --> 00:37:30.140 align:middle line:84%
And so then I suggested
a few other tweaks

00:37:30.140 --> 00:37:32.760 align:middle line:84%
based upon what he was
telling me about the industry,

00:37:32.760 --> 00:37:34.940 align:middle line:90%
the way it works in his field.

00:37:34.940 --> 00:37:38.940 align:middle line:84%
And we ran my version, and
it looked better still.

00:37:38.940 --> 00:37:43.220 align:middle line:84%
Over six months, I think we
ran 35 different versions

00:37:43.220 --> 00:37:45.460 align:middle line:90%
of this model.

00:37:45.460 --> 00:37:49.920 align:middle line:84%
And at the end of that six-month
period, he came into my office

00:37:49.920 --> 00:37:53.000 align:middle line:84%
and said, I just wanted to
let you know, I quit my job

00:37:53.000 --> 00:37:54.000 align:middle line:90%
yesterday.

00:37:54.000 --> 00:37:54.940 align:middle line:90%
I'm going to do this.

00:37:54.940 --> 00:37:56.770 align:middle line:90%
I'm going to build this company.

00:37:56.770 --> 00:37:59.020 align:middle line:84%
And I got to tell you, that
scared the hell out of me.

00:37:59.020 --> 00:38:01.540 align:middle line:84%
I had never had that effect
on any of my students.

00:38:01.540 --> 00:38:03.260 align:middle line:84%
And he had a young
kid in his household.

00:38:03.260 --> 00:38:05.860 align:middle line:84%
And I said, Neil, you
sure you want to do this?

00:38:05.860 --> 00:38:08.120 align:middle line:84%
Maybe you should let me
run a few more simulations.

00:38:08.120 --> 00:38:09.220 align:middle line:90%
And he said, no, no, no.

00:38:09.220 --> 00:38:09.887 align:middle line:90%
My wife is cool.

00:38:09.887 --> 00:38:11.480 align:middle line:90%
I think we're ready to do this.

00:38:11.480 --> 00:38:15.040 align:middle line:84%
I felt guilty enough that
I invested a small amount

00:38:15.040 --> 00:38:17.200 align:middle line:90%
in the friends-and-family round.

00:38:17.200 --> 00:38:18.960 align:middle line:84%
And in retrospect,
I wish I would

00:38:18.960 --> 00:38:23.880 align:middle line:84%
have invested a lot more because
that's probably my single most

00:38:23.880 --> 00:38:26.220 align:middle line:90%
profitable investment.

00:38:26.220 --> 00:38:28.660 align:middle line:84%
In 2017, this company,
BridgeBio Pharma,

00:38:28.660 --> 00:38:30.320 align:middle line:90%
came out of stealth mode.

00:38:30.320 --> 00:38:37.260 align:middle line:84%
And we had our first investor
was not a venture capitalist,

00:38:37.260 --> 00:38:40.720 align:middle line:84%
but a private equity firm
that prior to this company

00:38:40.720 --> 00:38:42.460 align:middle line:90%
had never invested in biotech.

00:38:42.460 --> 00:38:43.895 align:middle line:90%
The company is called KKR.

00:38:43.895 --> 00:38:45.020 align:middle line:90%
You may have heard of them.

00:38:45.020 --> 00:38:48.350 align:middle line:84%
Some of you undoubtedly will
be doing internships with them

00:38:48.350 --> 00:38:51.590 align:middle line:90%
sometime in the next few years.

00:38:51.590 --> 00:38:54.710 align:middle line:84%
We raised about $40 million
in that very first round

00:38:54.710 --> 00:38:56.810 align:middle line:84%
after the
friends-and-family round.

00:38:56.810 --> 00:38:59.870 align:middle line:84%
A year later, we
raised another 135,

00:38:59.870 --> 00:39:02.250 align:middle line:90%
a year later, yet another 300.

00:39:02.250 --> 00:39:05.610 align:middle line:84%
So we were right around the
$400 to $500 million range.

00:39:05.610 --> 00:39:08.930 align:middle line:84%
We had about 15 projects,
just like the simulations.

00:39:08.930 --> 00:39:11.650 align:middle line:84%
And at that point, we did
an IPO, and after the IPO,

00:39:11.650 --> 00:39:14.190 align:middle line:90%
we raised another 750.

00:39:14.190 --> 00:39:18.910 align:middle line:84%
And as of this morning,
or early this morning,

00:39:18.910 --> 00:39:21.110 align:middle line:84%
before the market
opened, BridgeBio

00:39:21.110 --> 00:39:25.550 align:middle line:84%
is currently trading at about
$4.6 billion of market cap.

00:39:25.550 --> 00:39:28.210 align:middle line:84%
All of the investors,
myself included,

00:39:28.210 --> 00:39:31.070 align:middle line:84%
have done better than we
ever would have expected,

00:39:31.070 --> 00:39:33.030 align:middle line:90%
a great rate of return.

00:39:33.030 --> 00:39:34.410 align:middle line:90%
But you know what?

00:39:34.410 --> 00:39:36.990 align:middle line:84%
That's not what this
company is proud of.

00:39:36.990 --> 00:39:39.110 align:middle line:84%
What they are most
proud of is the fact

00:39:39.110 --> 00:39:41.910 align:middle line:84%
that there are other
portfolio companies that have

00:39:41.910 --> 00:39:43.680 align:middle line:90%
gotten started, thanks to us.

00:39:43.680 --> 00:39:48.060 align:middle line:84%
And it's now become a thing that
you can use portfolio theory

00:39:48.060 --> 00:39:52.620 align:middle line:84%
to reduce your risk, as this
McKinsey report in 2021 put out.

00:39:52.620 --> 00:39:57.700 align:middle line:84%
And at this point, BridgeBio
has about 20 different projects

00:39:57.700 --> 00:39:58.798 align:middle line:90%
in the clinic.

00:39:58.798 --> 00:40:00.340 align:middle line:84%
Two drugs have
already been approved.

00:40:00.340 --> 00:40:04.940 align:middle line:84%
In 2021, a drug focusing
on a very rare kind

00:40:04.940 --> 00:40:11.280 align:middle line:84%
of enzyme deficiency, as well as
a rare kind of bile duct cancer.

00:40:11.280 --> 00:40:14.700 align:middle line:84%
Both of them were
approved in 2021.

00:40:14.700 --> 00:40:17.380 align:middle line:84%
A third blockbuster
drug looks like it's

00:40:17.380 --> 00:40:20.300 align:middle line:84%
going to get approved
sometime later this month.

00:40:20.300 --> 00:40:24.380 align:middle line:84%
So watch the news for an
announcement from BridgeBio.

00:40:24.380 --> 00:40:28.140 align:middle line:84%
And that gene therapy
for that little girl

00:40:28.140 --> 00:40:30.020 align:middle line:84%
who had Canavan
disease, that turns out

00:40:30.020 --> 00:40:31.980 align:middle line:90%
to be a BridgeBio drug.

00:40:31.980 --> 00:40:35.220 align:middle line:84%
And as of last year, that
drug with 10 patients

00:40:35.220 --> 00:40:37.900 align:middle line:84%
have shown efficacy in every
single one of the patients that

00:40:37.900 --> 00:40:39.020 align:middle line:90%
have the disease.

00:40:39.020 --> 00:40:42.650 align:middle line:84%
So we expect that the FDA will
approve that drug sometime

00:40:42.650 --> 00:40:44.090 align:middle line:90%
next year.

00:40:44.090 --> 00:40:45.650 align:middle line:90%
Really amazing.

00:40:45.650 --> 00:40:49.370 align:middle line:84%
And I got to tell you,
the proudest professional

00:40:49.370 --> 00:40:52.970 align:middle line:84%
accomplishment that
I've ever experienced

00:40:52.970 --> 00:40:57.450 align:middle line:84%
was this moment here when
I met that little girl.

00:40:57.450 --> 00:41:00.550 align:middle line:84%
She actually lives in
Boston in Jamaica Plain.

00:41:00.550 --> 00:41:02.370 align:middle line:90%
And here she was.

00:41:02.370 --> 00:41:05.450 align:middle line:84%
The parents found out
about the business model

00:41:05.450 --> 00:41:09.590 align:middle line:84%
that I had developed along with
Neil, and asked to meet with me.

00:41:09.590 --> 00:41:13.530 align:middle line:84%
And it was really kind of a
scary and extremely emotional

00:41:13.530 --> 00:41:14.610 align:middle line:90%
meeting.

00:41:14.610 --> 00:41:20.290 align:middle line:84%
This is the Flour Bakery right
across the Salt and Pepper

00:41:20.290 --> 00:41:23.617 align:middle line:90%
Bridge, where Mass General is.

00:41:23.617 --> 00:41:25.450 align:middle line:84%
That's where they were
going for their care.

00:41:25.450 --> 00:41:31.210 align:middle line:84%
And I went into this
Flour Bakery to meet them.

00:41:31.210 --> 00:41:36.290 align:middle line:84%
And my first vision was
walking up the stairs

00:41:36.290 --> 00:41:37.930 align:middle line:90%
holding her father's hand.

00:41:37.930 --> 00:41:39.760 align:middle line:84%
And she kept pushing
his hand away

00:41:39.760 --> 00:41:41.800 align:middle line:84%
because she wanted
to do it on her own.

00:41:41.800 --> 00:41:45.080 align:middle line:84%
It's just an extraordinary,
extraordinary moment.

00:41:45.080 --> 00:41:48.660 align:middle line:84%
This is the power of
financial engineering.

00:41:48.660 --> 00:41:50.440 align:middle line:90%
And let me be very clear.

00:41:50.440 --> 00:41:53.640 align:middle line:84%
I had zero to do with any
of the scientific decisions

00:41:53.640 --> 00:41:57.080 align:middle line:90%
that BridgeBio made.

00:41:57.080 --> 00:42:03.080 align:middle line:84%
I often tell people that
finance cannot turn a bad drug

00:42:03.080 --> 00:42:05.080 align:middle line:90%
into a good one.

00:42:05.080 --> 00:42:08.080 align:middle line:84%
But the lack of financing, or
the wrong kind of financing,

00:42:08.080 --> 00:42:10.960 align:middle line:84%
can easily take a good
drug, and destroy it,

00:42:10.960 --> 00:42:13.480 align:middle line:84%
and make sure it never
reaches a patient.

00:42:13.480 --> 00:42:19.640 align:middle line:84%
And so finance is an important
aspect of drug development,

00:42:19.640 --> 00:42:21.380 align:middle line:84%
but it is not the
most important aspect.

00:42:21.380 --> 00:42:22.922 align:middle line:84%
You've got to start
with the science,

00:42:22.922 --> 00:42:26.295 align:middle line:84%
and then figure out what
the right financing is.

00:42:26.295 --> 00:42:27.920 align:middle line:84%
So I'm going to turn
to my second topic

00:42:27.920 --> 00:42:30.020 align:middle line:90%
now, which is energy transition.

00:42:30.020 --> 00:42:33.520 align:middle line:84%
I want to illustrate the
fact that finance is not

00:42:33.520 --> 00:42:35.720 align:middle line:84%
just about biotech,
but it really

00:42:35.720 --> 00:42:37.990 align:middle line:90%
can transform any industry.

00:42:37.990 --> 00:42:40.990 align:middle line:84%
And I know a number of you are
concerned about climate change.

00:42:40.990 --> 00:42:43.710 align:middle line:90%
So am I. So is Jake and Peter.

00:42:43.710 --> 00:42:45.870 align:middle line:84%
And the question is,
what do we do about it?

00:42:45.870 --> 00:42:48.990 align:middle line:84%
Now there is this thing
called energy transition.

00:42:48.990 --> 00:42:51.630 align:middle line:84%
And there's a report that
I read a couple of years

00:42:51.630 --> 00:42:53.170 align:middle line:90%
ago that I highly recommend.

00:42:53.170 --> 00:42:56.670 align:middle line:84%
It's a rather harsh
dose of reality.

00:42:56.670 --> 00:42:58.870 align:middle line:84%
People talk about
energy transition

00:42:58.870 --> 00:43:02.990 align:middle line:84%
like we're going to get
off of fossil fuels soon.

00:43:02.990 --> 00:43:06.670 align:middle line:84%
And what this paper argues
is that that's really

00:43:06.670 --> 00:43:08.390 align:middle line:90%
not the right term.

00:43:08.390 --> 00:43:14.170 align:middle line:84%
We are not taking less and less
of our energy from fossil fuels.

00:43:14.170 --> 00:43:16.130 align:middle line:84%
And here's a graph
that illustrates it.

00:43:16.130 --> 00:43:19.390 align:middle line:84%
So this is a graph
of how much energy

00:43:19.390 --> 00:43:24.550 align:middle line:84%
we are consuming as broken
down by different sources.

00:43:24.550 --> 00:43:25.470 align:middle line:90%
So here.

00:43:25.470 --> 00:43:31.650 align:middle line:84%
Oil, you can see certainly
natural gas, and coal,

00:43:31.650 --> 00:43:37.940 align:middle line:84%
all three forms of fossil
fuels are growing in the amount

00:43:37.940 --> 00:43:41.287 align:middle line:84%
that we're consuming
year after year.

00:43:41.287 --> 00:43:42.120 align:middle line:90%
Now how can that be?

00:43:42.120 --> 00:43:44.113 align:middle line:84%
Because we are making
progress with renewables.

00:43:44.113 --> 00:43:44.940 align:middle line:90%
Right?

00:43:44.940 --> 00:43:46.520 align:middle line:90%
So here's wind and solar.

00:43:46.520 --> 00:43:48.900 align:middle line:90%
This is in blue.

00:43:48.900 --> 00:43:53.700 align:middle line:90%
And here is nuclear here.

00:43:53.700 --> 00:43:57.100 align:middle line:84%
They're getting a little
bit wider, but not

00:43:57.100 --> 00:44:00.940 align:middle line:84%
nearly as fast as
the fossil fuels are.

00:44:00.940 --> 00:44:05.180 align:middle line:84%
And it's because human
population is growing.

00:44:05.180 --> 00:44:09.980 align:middle line:84%
And also, the kind
of people that we

00:44:09.980 --> 00:44:12.140 align:middle line:84%
have on this planet,
that's changing.

00:44:12.140 --> 00:44:13.980 align:middle line:84%
We are now having
more and more people

00:44:13.980 --> 00:44:16.223 align:middle line:84%
that are moving into
the middle class.

00:44:16.223 --> 00:44:18.640 align:middle line:84%
And when you move into the
middle class, what do you want?

00:44:18.640 --> 00:44:19.920 align:middle line:90%
You want a better life.

00:44:19.920 --> 00:44:23.760 align:middle line:84%
You want a house, you want
two cars instead of one,

00:44:23.760 --> 00:44:26.660 align:middle line:84%
you want to take a vacation
by flying somewhere.

00:44:26.660 --> 00:44:28.500 align:middle line:84%
All of the things that
you and I take for

00:44:28.500 --> 00:44:31.900 align:middle line:84%
granted, there are
millions to hundreds

00:44:31.900 --> 00:44:34.650 align:middle line:84%
of millions of people that
are for the first time,

00:44:34.650 --> 00:44:36.550 align:middle line:90%
being able to experience it.

00:44:36.550 --> 00:44:39.250 align:middle line:84%
And that's why the
consumption of energy

00:44:39.250 --> 00:44:41.570 align:middle line:84%
is growing far faster
than our ability

00:44:41.570 --> 00:44:46.370 align:middle line:84%
to create non-greenhouse-gas
sources of energy.

00:44:46.370 --> 00:44:49.210 align:middle line:84%
So the problem with
energy transition

00:44:49.210 --> 00:44:52.830 align:middle line:84%
is that if you just
look at what's going on,

00:44:52.830 --> 00:44:56.530 align:middle line:84%
we can't just rely
on renewable energy.

00:44:56.530 --> 00:45:01.410 align:middle line:84%
We need to have low-carbon
forms of energy that

00:45:01.410 --> 00:45:03.770 align:middle line:84%
don't pollute the atmosphere,
and that are on demand,

00:45:03.770 --> 00:45:05.090 align:middle line:90%
available.

00:45:05.090 --> 00:45:07.250 align:middle line:90%
So what are we doing?

00:45:07.250 --> 00:45:11.970 align:middle line:84%
How are we transitioning from
one energy source to another?

00:45:11.970 --> 00:45:13.510 align:middle line:90%
It's not really transitioning.

00:45:13.510 --> 00:45:18.010 align:middle line:84%
We're not declining our usage
of these kinds of sources

00:45:18.010 --> 00:45:20.610 align:middle line:84%
of energy, but we need
to be adding to them

00:45:20.610 --> 00:45:23.010 align:middle line:90%
with low-carbon sources.

00:45:23.010 --> 00:45:27.450 align:middle line:84%
And so from what my experts
in energy, my colleagues here

00:45:27.450 --> 00:45:33.070 align:middle line:84%
in the geo department
are telling me,

00:45:33.070 --> 00:45:38.550 align:middle line:84%
they're telling me that there
are three sources of energy that

00:45:38.550 --> 00:45:39.570 align:middle line:90%
is non-renewable.

00:45:39.570 --> 00:45:43.310 align:middle line:84%
They're not wind and solar,
but they are ultimately

00:45:43.310 --> 00:45:46.190 align:middle line:84%
going to be infinite,
close-to-infinite,

00:45:46.190 --> 00:45:47.250 align:middle line:90%
sources of energy.

00:45:47.250 --> 00:45:50.990 align:middle line:84%
And that is fusion,
fission, and geothermal.

00:45:50.990 --> 00:45:52.910 align:middle line:84%
And for a variety
of reasons, we're

00:45:52.910 --> 00:45:55.390 align:middle line:84%
not going to see
a lot of progress

00:45:55.390 --> 00:45:57.270 align:middle line:90%
in the near term for fission.

00:45:57.270 --> 00:45:59.510 align:middle line:84%
We already have
nuclear power plants.

00:45:59.510 --> 00:46:02.070 align:middle line:84%
But the problem from a
political point of view

00:46:02.070 --> 00:46:04.510 align:middle line:84%
is nobody wants to have
them near where they live.

00:46:04.510 --> 00:46:08.270 align:middle line:84%
And nobody wants to deal
with the mess of the fuels

00:46:08.270 --> 00:46:10.850 align:middle line:84%
that ultimately,
when they degrade,

00:46:10.850 --> 00:46:13.510 align:middle line:84%
become radioactive
and dangerous.

00:46:13.510 --> 00:46:18.930 align:middle line:84%
So because the prices of fossil
fuels are highly path dependent,

00:46:18.930 --> 00:46:22.390 align:middle line:84%
this energy transition is very,
very difficult to predict.

00:46:22.390 --> 00:46:24.070 align:middle line:84%
And what that
tells us is we need

00:46:24.070 --> 00:46:26.990 align:middle line:84%
to start thinking about
alternative sources of energy

00:46:26.990 --> 00:46:29.670 align:middle line:84%
that are going to
be sustainable.

00:46:29.670 --> 00:46:32.020 align:middle line:84%
And so it turns out
that nuclear energy

00:46:32.020 --> 00:46:34.820 align:middle line:84%
is the main source
of sustainable

00:46:34.820 --> 00:46:40.620 align:middle line:84%
energy for the long-term
future of the human species.

00:46:40.620 --> 00:46:43.420 align:middle line:84%
And there are only two forms
of nuclear energy, fusion

00:46:43.420 --> 00:46:44.380 align:middle line:90%
and fission.

00:46:44.380 --> 00:46:47.260 align:middle line:84%
So as you probably know, fusion
is when we split an atom,

00:46:47.260 --> 00:46:50.860 align:middle line:84%
and you get more energy
out than you put in.

00:46:50.860 --> 00:46:55.505 align:middle line:84%
But fusion energy is where
you squeeze atoms together.

00:46:55.505 --> 00:46:57.380 align:middle line:84%
And it turns out that
the squeezing gives you

00:46:57.380 --> 00:47:00.780 align:middle line:84%
a lot more energy than
the splitting of an atom.

00:47:00.780 --> 00:47:02.820 align:middle line:84%
It also turns out
that fusion energy

00:47:02.820 --> 00:47:06.720 align:middle line:84%
is the most abundant form of
energy in the entire universe.

00:47:06.720 --> 00:47:07.820 align:middle line:90%
Why?

00:47:07.820 --> 00:47:10.620 align:middle line:84%
Because this is
what powers stars,

00:47:10.620 --> 00:47:12.920 align:middle line:84%
and that's where we
get all of our energy

00:47:12.920 --> 00:47:14.280 align:middle line:90%
other than geothermal.

00:47:14.280 --> 00:47:17.980 align:middle line:84%
It's basically we're
getting energy from the sun.

00:47:17.980 --> 00:47:20.380 align:middle line:84%
So fusion, turns
out, can produce

00:47:20.380 --> 00:47:25.740 align:middle line:84%
roughly 4 times more energy per
kilogram of fuel than fission.

00:47:25.740 --> 00:47:29.770 align:middle line:84%
And here's the kicker
it does not produce

00:47:29.770 --> 00:47:32.210 align:middle line:90%
long-lasting radioactive waste.

00:47:32.210 --> 00:47:38.250 align:middle line:84%
When you are finished using
fission-based energy systems

00:47:38.250 --> 00:47:42.170 align:middle line:84%
like uranium, plutonium, you
will have radioactive waste that

00:47:42.170 --> 00:47:46.010 align:middle line:84%
in some cases have a half
life of 100,000 years,

00:47:46.010 --> 00:47:48.530 align:middle line:84%
which is a lot longer
than I plan to live.

00:47:48.530 --> 00:47:50.030 align:middle line:90%
So that's the problem.

00:47:50.030 --> 00:47:56.890 align:middle line:84%
Whereas with fusion energy,
it produces water, and helium,

00:47:56.890 --> 00:47:59.490 align:middle line:84%
and maybe some tritium,
which is radioactive.

00:47:59.490 --> 00:48:00.910 align:middle line:90%
But it doesn't last that long.

00:48:00.910 --> 00:48:02.327 align:middle line:84%
And moreover,
tritium is something

00:48:02.327 --> 00:48:07.610 align:middle line:84%
that we need as part
of our fusion inputs.

00:48:07.610 --> 00:48:13.090 align:middle line:84%
So the problem with fusion is
you need three things to happen,

00:48:13.090 --> 00:48:16.930 align:middle line:84%
the so-called Lawson
triple product.

00:48:16.930 --> 00:48:20.090 align:middle line:84%
You need to take a
compound, some matter,

00:48:20.090 --> 00:48:22.770 align:middle line:84%
and you need to squeeze
it together really

00:48:22.770 --> 00:48:28.120 align:middle line:84%
hard because that's how you're
going to get atoms to fuse.

00:48:28.120 --> 00:48:32.160 align:middle line:84%
Unfortunately, just squeezing
it is not enough on this planet.

00:48:32.160 --> 00:48:36.060 align:middle line:84%
You have to loosen them up so
that they will come together.

00:48:36.060 --> 00:48:38.800 align:middle line:84%
And that means you have
to heat it to a very, very

00:48:38.800 --> 00:48:40.440 align:middle line:90%
high temperature.

00:48:40.440 --> 00:48:43.400 align:middle line:84%
And then you have
to hold on to it

00:48:43.400 --> 00:48:48.160 align:middle line:84%
for a long enough period of time
so that the fusion reactions can

00:48:48.160 --> 00:48:49.200 align:middle line:90%
happen.

00:48:49.200 --> 00:48:50.680 align:middle line:84%
So these are the
three things that

00:48:50.680 --> 00:48:53.800 align:middle line:84%
have to happen, temperature,
pressure, and time.

00:48:53.800 --> 00:48:57.680 align:middle line:84%
Now it turns out that on
the sun, or in the sun,

00:48:57.680 --> 00:48:59.540 align:middle line:90%
this happens relatively easily.

00:48:59.540 --> 00:49:03.800 align:middle line:84%
The pressure gets created
by gravitational pull.

00:49:03.800 --> 00:49:07.160 align:middle line:84%
And the temperature
increases as a function

00:49:07.160 --> 00:49:09.700 align:middle line:84%
of how it pulls in
through the gravity,

00:49:09.700 --> 00:49:12.080 align:middle line:90%
the different combustible items.

00:49:12.080 --> 00:49:16.280 align:middle line:84%
And as we know, once
you ignite fusion,

00:49:16.280 --> 00:49:19.680 align:middle line:84%
it lasts, and lasts, and
lasts for many, many hundreds

00:49:19.680 --> 00:49:23.360 align:middle line:84%
of thousands to
millions of years.

00:49:23.360 --> 00:49:28.910 align:middle line:84%
So something pretty amazing
happened here in Cambridge

00:49:28.910 --> 00:49:32.430 align:middle line:90%
on Labor Day weekend, 2021.

00:49:32.430 --> 00:49:35.950 align:middle line:84%
What happened is that
MIT, in collaboration

00:49:35.950 --> 00:49:41.070 align:middle line:84%
with a spin off that it created
called Commonwealth Fusion

00:49:41.070 --> 00:49:46.590 align:middle line:84%
Systems, they created
a magnetic field

00:49:46.590 --> 00:49:50.950 align:middle line:84%
that was so strong, that
it will, in principle, be

00:49:50.950 --> 00:49:56.070 align:middle line:84%
able to contain fusion
reactions to the degree

00:49:56.070 --> 00:49:59.510 align:middle line:84%
that we need in order
to achieve ignition.

00:49:59.510 --> 00:50:03.470 align:middle line:84%
It can actually
light this plasma.

00:50:03.470 --> 00:50:09.830 align:middle line:84%
And this demonstration occurred
about five blocks from here

00:50:09.830 --> 00:50:12.190 align:middle line:90%
on Albany Street.

00:50:12.190 --> 00:50:14.490 align:middle line:90%
They created a 20-tesla magnet.

00:50:14.490 --> 00:50:16.410 align:middle line:90%
20-tesla magnet.

00:50:16.410 --> 00:50:19.310 align:middle line:84%
That is an extremely
strong magnetic field.

00:50:19.310 --> 00:50:22.270 align:middle line:84%
And the interesting thing
about what happened here

00:50:22.270 --> 00:50:25.580 align:middle line:90%
is that three months later--

00:50:25.580 --> 00:50:27.440 align:middle line:84%
and by the way,
this is yours truly.

00:50:27.440 --> 00:50:28.860 align:middle line:90%
I was there for this event.

00:50:28.860 --> 00:50:31.580 align:middle line:84%
I collaborated with Dennis
Whyte, the head of the Plasma

00:50:31.580 --> 00:50:33.140 align:middle line:90%
Science and Fusion Center.

00:50:33.140 --> 00:50:34.920 align:middle line:84%
So he kindly invited
me to be there.

00:50:34.920 --> 00:50:38.660 align:middle line:84%
This was, by the way, Sunday
morning at 7:30 AM Labor Day

00:50:38.660 --> 00:50:39.540 align:middle line:90%
weekend.

00:50:39.540 --> 00:50:41.820 align:middle line:84%
I was out on the
Cape with my family.

00:50:41.820 --> 00:50:43.840 align:middle line:84%
And I abandoned
them to come here.

00:50:43.840 --> 00:50:45.700 align:middle line:90%
That's how nerdy I was.

00:50:45.700 --> 00:50:48.620 align:middle line:84%
But it was an extraordinary
historical event.

00:50:48.620 --> 00:50:51.620 align:middle line:84%
What happened after
that, of course, was we

00:50:51.620 --> 00:50:58.180 align:middle line:84%
saw that the Lawrence
Livermore Laboratory, Berkeley,

00:50:58.180 --> 00:51:01.500 align:middle line:84%
they actually created a
truly exothermic fusion

00:51:01.500 --> 00:51:04.540 align:middle line:84%
reaction, meaning
more power came out

00:51:04.540 --> 00:51:08.660 align:middle line:84%
than money went than energy went
in, which basically says you're

00:51:08.660 --> 00:51:10.560 align:middle line:90%
creating money out of nothing.

00:51:10.560 --> 00:51:14.440 align:middle line:84%
If money is energy, then
you're putting more money--

00:51:14.440 --> 00:51:18.380 align:middle line:84%
you're getting more money
out than you're putting in.

00:51:18.380 --> 00:51:20.180 align:middle line:84%
Also, the regulatory
authorities have

00:51:20.180 --> 00:51:23.170 align:middle line:84%
agreed that fusion should not be
regulated the way that fission

00:51:23.170 --> 00:51:24.890 align:middle line:90%
is, which is pretty big.

00:51:24.890 --> 00:51:29.890 align:middle line:84%
And yet another repeat
of fusion in 2023.

00:51:29.890 --> 00:51:34.370 align:middle line:84%
So the question that my
colleague in the Plasma Science

00:51:34.370 --> 00:51:36.690 align:middle line:84%
and Fusion Center, Dennis
Whyte, and I have asked

00:51:36.690 --> 00:51:40.590 align:middle line:84%
is, can we use finance
to fund fusion?

00:51:40.590 --> 00:51:43.250 align:middle line:84%
So we wrote a paper
about this too.

00:51:43.250 --> 00:51:45.970 align:middle line:84%
And in this paper,
we do a calculation

00:51:45.970 --> 00:51:50.770 align:middle line:84%
that shows, yes, there is a path
of creating a portfolio in order

00:51:50.770 --> 00:51:51.550 align:middle line:90%
to fund fusion.

00:51:51.550 --> 00:51:53.450 align:middle line:90%
Now a portfolio of what?

00:51:53.450 --> 00:51:56.850 align:middle line:84%
So I have to answer
that question.

00:51:56.850 --> 00:51:59.290 align:middle line:84%
In order for me to answer
a question about portfolio

00:51:59.290 --> 00:52:00.930 align:middle line:84%
of what, I have to
describe, what do I

00:52:00.930 --> 00:52:03.970 align:middle line:90%
mean by saying, fusion?

00:52:03.970 --> 00:52:06.250 align:middle line:90%
What do we mean by fusion?

00:52:06.250 --> 00:52:10.570 align:middle line:84%
What I mean is to create a
commercially-viable power

00:52:10.570 --> 00:52:17.170 align:middle line:84%
plant, a reactor, that will
generate enough heat to create

00:52:17.170 --> 00:52:21.200 align:middle line:84%
a turbine that will spin
and generate electrons

00:52:21.200 --> 00:52:22.940 align:middle line:90%
to be put on the power grid.

00:52:22.940 --> 00:52:25.520 align:middle line:90%
That's what I call, fusion.

00:52:25.520 --> 00:52:27.920 align:middle line:84%
And so what that means
is you need this.

00:52:27.920 --> 00:52:31.760 align:middle line:84%
You need a fusion reactor
that will do its thing

00:52:31.760 --> 00:52:33.440 align:middle line:90%
and generate heat.

00:52:33.440 --> 00:52:37.160 align:middle line:84%
The heat will basically heat up
water, water will turn to steam.

00:52:37.160 --> 00:52:40.560 align:middle line:84%
That will basically
cause turbines to spin.

00:52:40.560 --> 00:52:43.080 align:middle line:84%
As the turbines spin,
you generate electricity,

00:52:43.080 --> 00:52:46.080 align:middle line:84%
and the electricity goes
on existing power grids.

00:52:46.080 --> 00:52:51.320 align:middle line:84%
So this whole thing is
what I'm calling, fusion.

00:52:51.320 --> 00:52:52.780 align:middle line:90%
That's what I want to do.

00:52:52.780 --> 00:52:55.720 align:middle line:90%
That's what I want to invest in.

00:52:55.720 --> 00:52:57.160 align:middle line:90%
Now how do we do that?

00:52:57.160 --> 00:53:00.080 align:middle line:90%
How do we invest in fusion?

00:53:00.080 --> 00:53:02.640 align:middle line:84%
And the answer has
to do with what

00:53:02.640 --> 00:53:06.600 align:middle line:84%
it takes to achieve fusion
electrons on the grid.

00:53:06.600 --> 00:53:09.120 align:middle line:84%
These are all the
components that experts

00:53:09.120 --> 00:53:14.080 align:middle line:84%
tell me are needed in order
to get fusion to happen.

00:53:14.080 --> 00:53:21.060 align:middle line:84%
And so the question
is, how do we do that?

00:53:21.060 --> 00:53:23.120 align:middle line:84%
And the answer,
like anything else,

00:53:23.120 --> 00:53:26.820 align:middle line:84%
is break it apart,
look at it one by one,

00:53:26.820 --> 00:53:28.660 align:middle line:84%
and figure out what
you need in order

00:53:28.660 --> 00:53:30.900 align:middle line:84%
to achieve these
different components.

00:53:30.900 --> 00:53:34.020 align:middle line:84%
So one of the things
we need is sustained

00:53:34.020 --> 00:53:36.140 align:middle line:90%
high-temperature plasma.

00:53:36.140 --> 00:53:39.720 align:middle line:84%
So plasma is a fourth
state of matter.

00:53:39.720 --> 00:53:42.360 align:middle line:84%
When you heat something up
to a certain temperature,

00:53:42.360 --> 00:53:44.460 align:middle line:90%
it becomes plasma.

00:53:44.460 --> 00:53:46.080 align:middle line:84%
The problem is as
it gets hotter,

00:53:46.080 --> 00:53:51.460 align:middle line:84%
it's harder and harder to
contain this kind of matter.

00:53:51.460 --> 00:53:53.840 align:middle line:84%
And so you need to have
some way to hold on to it.

00:53:53.840 --> 00:53:57.660 align:middle line:84%
Well, magnetic bottles
are what we're considering

00:53:57.660 --> 00:53:59.380 align:middle line:90%
in one form of fusion.

00:53:59.380 --> 00:54:03.380 align:middle line:84%
But if that's the
issue of creating

00:54:03.380 --> 00:54:06.260 align:middle line:84%
high-temperature
plasma, then let's

00:54:06.260 --> 00:54:07.680 align:middle line:90%
take it and break it apart.

00:54:07.680 --> 00:54:10.820 align:middle line:90%
What do we need just to do that?

00:54:10.820 --> 00:54:12.900 align:middle line:84%
And by the way,
just to let you know

00:54:12.900 --> 00:54:14.580 align:middle line:84%
what high temperature
means, we're

00:54:14.580 --> 00:54:18.210 align:middle line:84%
talking about 100
million degrees.

00:54:18.210 --> 00:54:20.750 align:middle line:90%
100 million degrees.

00:54:20.750 --> 00:54:22.690 align:middle line:90%
That's hot.

00:54:22.690 --> 00:54:24.085 align:middle line:90%
So how do we achieve that?

00:54:24.085 --> 00:54:26.210 align:middle line:84%
Well, it turns out that
there are a bunch of things

00:54:26.210 --> 00:54:29.370 align:middle line:90%
you need in order to do it.

00:54:29.370 --> 00:54:30.730 align:middle line:90%
So what do we do?

00:54:30.730 --> 00:54:33.210 align:middle line:84%
This is the portfolio
I'm talking about.

00:54:33.210 --> 00:54:37.050 align:middle line:84%
I'm talking about creating
a portfolio of technology

00:54:37.050 --> 00:54:42.290 align:middle line:84%
that, when combined, will
allow you to achieve fusion.

00:54:42.290 --> 00:54:46.750 align:middle line:84%
But even if never combined, they
have value in and of itself.

00:54:46.750 --> 00:54:49.110 align:middle line:84%
For example, advanced
vacuum systems.

00:54:49.110 --> 00:54:51.370 align:middle line:84%
It turns out that
in order to achieve

00:54:51.370 --> 00:54:53.250 align:middle line:84%
really high
temperatures, you have

00:54:53.250 --> 00:54:58.950 align:middle line:84%
to have ultra-pure conditions
with no contaminants and no air,

00:54:58.950 --> 00:54:59.930 align:middle line:90%
in particular.

00:54:59.930 --> 00:55:01.730 align:middle line:84%
You need to have a
vacuum system that

00:55:01.730 --> 00:55:05.610 align:middle line:84%
works far better than your
typical Hoover vacuum cleaner.

00:55:05.610 --> 00:55:08.430 align:middle line:84%
It turns out that if you can
create that vacuum system,

00:55:08.430 --> 00:55:11.790 align:middle line:84%
it has applications in many
other things, like for example,

00:55:11.790 --> 00:55:14.000 align:middle line:90%
in drug development.

00:55:14.000 --> 00:55:18.800 align:middle line:84%
And so if you think about
fusion not as one thing,

00:55:18.800 --> 00:55:23.360 align:middle line:84%
but as a portfolio of a whole
bunch of different technologies

00:55:23.360 --> 00:55:25.680 align:middle line:84%
that have value in
and of themselves,

00:55:25.680 --> 00:55:28.880 align:middle line:84%
but that when combined,
will have value for fusion,

00:55:28.880 --> 00:55:31.440 align:middle line:84%
you can basically
break it apart and do

00:55:31.440 --> 00:55:33.880 align:middle line:84%
the kind of financial
analysis that we just

00:55:33.880 --> 00:55:37.200 align:middle line:84%
did with a single drug
development project,

00:55:37.200 --> 00:55:41.400 align:middle line:84%
and then put them into a
portfolio, maybe 150 of them,

00:55:41.400 --> 00:55:43.800 align:middle line:84%
and figure out the
risk and reward,

00:55:43.800 --> 00:55:46.080 align:middle line:90%
and be able to finance it.

00:55:46.080 --> 00:55:50.200 align:middle line:84%
So the idea is to create a
multibillion dollar fusion

00:55:50.200 --> 00:55:55.000 align:middle line:84%
mega fund, just like we did
with the cancer mega fund,

00:55:55.000 --> 00:55:57.720 align:middle line:84%
and finance all these
different technologies.

00:55:57.720 --> 00:56:01.292 align:middle line:84%
And imagine creating an
incredible board of advisors.

00:56:01.292 --> 00:56:03.000 align:middle line:84%
On the scientific
side, you've got people

00:56:03.000 --> 00:56:07.640 align:middle line:84%
like Mike Campbell, University
of Rochester expert in laser

00:56:07.640 --> 00:56:11.230 align:middle line:84%
technology, Steve Chu, former
energy secretary, Nobel Prize

00:56:11.230 --> 00:56:15.710 align:middle line:84%
winner, Rob Goldston, Dave
Larbalestier, Ernest Moniz, also

00:56:15.710 --> 00:56:18.550 align:middle line:84%
another former Energy Secretary
that was an MIT faculty

00:56:18.550 --> 00:56:21.910 align:middle line:84%
member, Dennis Whyte, the former
head of the Plasma Science

00:56:21.910 --> 00:56:23.670 align:middle line:90%
and Fusion Center, and so on.

00:56:23.670 --> 00:56:25.590 align:middle line:84%
And then on the
business side, you've

00:56:25.590 --> 00:56:28.690 align:middle line:84%
got people like Sam Altman,
Jeff Bezos, Peter Diamandis,

00:56:28.690 --> 00:56:31.710 align:middle line:84%
Bill Gates, Vinod Khosla,
so on, and so forth,

00:56:31.710 --> 00:56:37.230 align:middle line:84%
people that can actually help
us manage the business models.

00:56:37.230 --> 00:56:40.008 align:middle line:84%
Now you might say,
well, wait a minute.

00:56:40.008 --> 00:56:41.550 align:middle line:84%
How are you going
to get these people

00:56:41.550 --> 00:56:44.270 align:middle line:90%
to be part of this effort?

00:56:44.270 --> 00:56:48.470 align:middle line:84%
And that's a challenge,
because the folks in blue,

00:56:48.470 --> 00:56:50.870 align:middle line:90%
they're not motivated by money.

00:56:50.870 --> 00:56:54.310 align:middle line:84%
So if I call them up and
ask them to be part of this,

00:56:54.310 --> 00:56:58.550 align:middle line:84%
I would doubt that they would
even return my phone calls.

00:56:58.550 --> 00:57:00.590 align:middle line:90%
They've never heard of me.

00:57:00.590 --> 00:57:04.630 align:middle line:84%
The folks in green,
they have all the money.

00:57:04.630 --> 00:57:09.860 align:middle line:84%
And so I'm pretty sure that, and
actually I know a number of them

00:57:09.860 --> 00:57:11.100 align:middle line:90%
already.

00:57:11.100 --> 00:57:13.240 align:middle line:84%
So that's why I know
that if I call them,

00:57:13.240 --> 00:57:15.340 align:middle line:84%
they will not return
my phone calls.

00:57:15.340 --> 00:57:20.360 align:middle line:84%
But if you raise multi-billion
dollars to deploy capital,

00:57:20.360 --> 00:57:22.270 align:middle line:84%
all of them will
return your phone call,

00:57:22.270 --> 00:57:23.560 align:middle line:90%
not just because of the money.

00:57:23.560 --> 00:57:26.078 align:middle line:84%
As I said, the people in blue,
they don't care about money.

00:57:26.078 --> 00:57:28.120 align:middle line:84%
And the people in green,
they have all the money.

00:57:28.120 --> 00:57:29.245 align:middle line:90%
They don't need your money.

00:57:29.245 --> 00:57:31.700 align:middle line:84%
But if you can actually
show that there's

00:57:31.700 --> 00:57:34.780 align:middle line:84%
a business path that
allows you to achieve

00:57:34.780 --> 00:57:39.580 align:middle line:84%
your goal of ignition, they'll
all want to be part of it.

00:57:39.580 --> 00:57:42.980 align:middle line:84%
And finally, how many of you, if
I created a fund like this with

00:57:42.980 --> 00:57:45.980 align:middle line:84%
these people, how many of
you would put as a one-time

00:57:45.980 --> 00:57:49.020 align:middle line:84%
investment, just
one-time investment,

00:57:49.020 --> 00:57:54.620 align:middle line:84%
$1,500 of your 401(k) money
that you may have saved up after

00:57:54.620 --> 00:57:57.620 align:middle line:84%
a summer internship or after
your first year working

00:57:57.620 --> 00:57:58.240 align:middle line:90%
at your job?

00:57:58.240 --> 00:58:03.620 align:middle line:84%
How many of you would put
1,500 into a fusion fund?

00:58:03.620 --> 00:58:07.000 align:middle line:84%
That's where I'm going to get
the multi-billions of dollars,

00:58:07.000 --> 00:58:10.270 align:middle line:84%
because with 130 million
households out there,

00:58:10.270 --> 00:58:15.130 align:middle line:84%
if 3% of you put in $1,500,
that's more than $30 billion

00:58:15.130 --> 00:58:15.930 align:middle line:90%
right there.

00:58:15.930 --> 00:58:17.330 align:middle line:90%
We can do this.

00:58:17.330 --> 00:58:20.570 align:middle line:90%
This can actually be done.

00:58:20.570 --> 00:58:22.510 align:middle line:84%
So there are many
other examples of this.

00:58:22.510 --> 00:58:23.010 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:58:23.010 --> 00:58:24.290 align:middle line:90%
Question.

00:58:24.290 --> 00:58:26.690 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Wouldn't the
portfolio for the fusion of all

00:58:26.690 --> 00:58:27.730 align:middle line:84%
those different
things, wouldn't they

00:58:27.730 --> 00:58:30.410 align:middle line:84%
be highly correlated these
different industries as opposed

00:58:30.410 --> 00:58:31.877 align:middle line:90%
to the drugs [INAUDIBLE]?

00:58:31.877 --> 00:58:33.710 align:middle line:84%
ANDREW W. LO: Well,
that's a great question.

00:58:33.710 --> 00:58:35.370 align:middle line:84%
Let's go back and
take a look at it.

00:58:35.370 --> 00:58:39.170 align:middle line:84%
So it's true that
the entire fusion

00:58:39.170 --> 00:58:43.690 align:middle line:84%
reactors of many different
companies will be correlated.

00:58:43.690 --> 00:58:45.703 align:middle line:84%
But they will not be
perfectly correlated

00:58:45.703 --> 00:58:47.870 align:middle line:84%
if they're using different
ways of achieving fusion.

00:58:47.870 --> 00:58:50.763 align:middle line:84%
One uses magnetic, the
other one uses inertial.

00:58:50.763 --> 00:58:52.430 align:middle line:84%
And so there are
different technologies.

00:58:52.430 --> 00:58:55.850 align:middle line:84%
But more importantly, if
I look at this portfolio

00:58:55.850 --> 00:58:59.350 align:middle line:84%
of technologies, so one of
them is plasma heating systems,

00:58:59.350 --> 00:59:00.890 align:middle line:90%
how to make things really hot.

00:59:00.890 --> 00:59:03.010 align:middle line:84%
The second one is
advanced vacuum systems,

00:59:03.010 --> 00:59:05.130 align:middle line:84%
how to keep things
really clean and pure.

00:59:05.130 --> 00:59:08.853 align:middle line:84%
A third one is magnetic
confinement technologies,

00:59:08.853 --> 00:59:10.520 align:middle line:84%
so how do you shape
the magnets in order

00:59:10.520 --> 00:59:14.280 align:middle line:84%
to keep the bottle
from becoming unstable.

00:59:14.280 --> 00:59:17.267 align:middle line:84%
Each of those things have
nothing to do with each other.

00:59:17.267 --> 00:59:18.100 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Oh, right.

00:59:18.100 --> 00:59:18.600 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:59:18.600 --> 00:59:19.580 align:middle line:90%
I thought they might.

00:59:19.580 --> 00:59:20.198 align:middle line:90%
That's all.

00:59:20.198 --> 00:59:21.240 align:middle line:90%
OK, that makes sense now.

00:59:21.240 --> 00:59:23.760 align:middle line:84%
ANDREW W. LO: Yeah, the
technology, some of them do.

00:59:23.760 --> 00:59:25.645 align:middle line:90%
But most of them do not.

00:59:25.645 --> 00:59:28.020 align:middle line:84%
And that's where you're going
to get the diversification.

00:59:28.020 --> 00:59:30.680 align:middle line:90%
You're right.

00:59:30.680 --> 00:59:33.800 align:middle line:90%
So lots of other technologies.

00:59:33.800 --> 00:59:37.380 align:middle line:84%
The key to all of these are
that they are long shots.

00:59:37.380 --> 00:59:40.920 align:middle line:84%
Very, very low probability, or
unknown probability, of success.

00:59:40.920 --> 00:59:43.860 align:middle line:84%
They cost a lot of money,
they take a lot of time,

00:59:43.860 --> 00:59:46.960 align:middle line:84%
but the returns to
society are huge.

00:59:46.960 --> 00:59:50.360 align:middle line:84%
And so that's why you need
to have innovative financing

00:59:50.360 --> 00:59:51.840 align:middle line:90%
for these long shots.

00:59:51.840 --> 00:59:56.080 align:middle line:84%
The key to all of this is
to have the right narrative,

00:59:56.080 --> 00:59:57.782 align:middle line:84%
the right story
to tell investors,

00:59:57.782 --> 00:59:59.240 align:middle line:84%
about how you're
going to give them

00:59:59.240 --> 01:00:01.160 align:middle line:90%
a return on their investment.

01:00:01.160 --> 01:00:03.640 align:middle line:84%
And that requires the
right business models

01:00:03.640 --> 01:00:05.750 align:middle line:90%
to be able to do that.

01:00:05.750 --> 01:00:09.410 align:middle line:84%
So I'm going to wrap up with a
story that I use lots of times,

01:00:09.410 --> 01:00:14.030 align:middle line:84%
because when I first heard it, I
was so impressed that I decided

01:00:14.030 --> 01:00:16.950 align:middle line:84%
that that was going to be my
motivation for doing what I'm

01:00:16.950 --> 01:00:18.630 align:middle line:90%
doing.

01:00:18.630 --> 01:00:21.090 align:middle line:84%
It has to do with this man,
Professor Harvey Lodish.

01:00:21.090 --> 01:00:23.710 align:middle line:84%
He's a biology faculty
member here at MIT

01:00:23.710 --> 01:00:26.150 align:middle line:90%
in the Whitehead Institute.

01:00:26.150 --> 01:00:29.650 align:middle line:84%
Harvey, when he was an
assistant professor in 1983,

01:00:29.650 --> 01:00:33.550 align:middle line:84%
he was approached by
a businessman asking

01:00:33.550 --> 01:00:36.430 align:middle line:84%
whether he would collaborate
with him to create

01:00:36.430 --> 01:00:40.710 align:middle line:84%
a treatment for a rare disease
called Gaucher's syndrome.

01:00:40.710 --> 01:00:43.350 align:middle line:84%
This is also one of these
genetic typos, which

01:00:43.350 --> 01:00:44.870 align:middle line:84%
prevents your body
from producing

01:00:44.870 --> 01:00:46.490 align:middle line:90%
important housekeeping enzymes.

01:00:46.490 --> 01:00:48.590 align:middle line:84%
And without this
enzyme, fatty acids

01:00:48.590 --> 01:00:52.750 align:middle line:84%
build up in your spleen,
your bone marrow.

01:00:52.750 --> 01:00:54.370 align:middle line:84%
And by the time
you're a teenager,

01:00:54.370 --> 01:00:56.890 align:middle line:84%
if you have a very serious
version of this disease,

01:00:56.890 --> 01:00:58.710 align:middle line:90%
you're dead.

01:00:58.710 --> 01:01:00.590 align:middle line:84%
And so Harvey and
his colleagues, they

01:01:00.590 --> 01:01:03.460 align:middle line:84%
developed the treatment
to deal with this disease

01:01:03.460 --> 01:01:07.060 align:middle line:84%
by replacing the enzyme with an
artificially-developed version

01:01:07.060 --> 01:01:08.060 align:middle line:90%
of it.

01:01:08.060 --> 01:01:13.060 align:middle line:84%
And this little
company in 1991 got

01:01:13.060 --> 01:01:16.400 align:middle line:84%
a drug called Ceredase
approved by the FDA,

01:01:16.400 --> 01:01:19.760 align:middle line:84%
the first-ever enzyme
replacement therapy.

01:01:19.760 --> 01:01:23.820 align:middle line:84%
They would just put back into
these individuals' bodies

01:01:23.820 --> 01:01:27.400 align:middle line:84%
the missing enzyme that
their bodies don't produce.

01:01:27.400 --> 01:01:30.100 align:middle line:84%
They can produce it outside,
and give it to them.

01:01:30.100 --> 01:01:34.220 align:middle line:84%
And you may have heard of
Harvey's little company.

01:01:34.220 --> 01:01:35.780 align:middle line:90%
It's called Genzyme.

01:01:35.780 --> 01:01:41.460 align:middle line:84%
And in 2014, Genzyme
was purchased by Sanofi

01:01:41.460 --> 01:01:42.700 align:middle line:90%
for $20 billion.

01:01:42.700 --> 01:01:45.820 align:middle line:90%


01:01:45.820 --> 01:01:48.540 align:middle line:84%
Now that's not why I want to be
Harvey Lodish, although that's

01:01:48.540 --> 01:01:50.040 align:middle line:90%
not a bad reason, by the way.

01:01:50.040 --> 01:01:52.140 align:middle line:90%
I'm an economist, after all.

01:01:52.140 --> 01:01:55.540 align:middle line:84%
See, I want to be Harvey because
of what happened in 2002.

01:01:55.540 --> 01:01:57.980 align:middle line:84%
In that year,
Harvey's daughter was

01:01:57.980 --> 01:02:00.300 align:middle line:84%
pregnant with her
second child, Harvey

01:02:00.300 --> 01:02:04.000 align:middle line:84%
and his wife's second
grandchild, a boy named Andrew.

01:02:04.000 --> 01:02:06.520 align:middle line:90%
Great name, by the way.

01:02:06.520 --> 01:02:10.710 align:middle line:84%
Andrew was diagnosed in
utero with Gaucher syndrome.

01:02:10.710 --> 01:02:13.400 align:middle line:90%


01:02:13.400 --> 01:02:16.240 align:middle line:90%
What are the chances of that?

01:02:16.240 --> 01:02:17.980 align:middle line:84%
So I talked to
Harvey about this.

01:02:17.980 --> 01:02:19.718 align:middle line:90%
I asked him.

01:02:19.718 --> 01:02:21.260 align:middle line:84%
It was a very
emotional conversation.

01:02:21.260 --> 01:02:25.240 align:middle line:84%
I asked him, Harvey, did you
know in 1983, when you started

01:02:25.240 --> 01:02:29.440 align:middle line:84%
Genzyme, that you would
be developing a drug that

01:02:29.440 --> 01:02:36.480 align:middle line:84%
would someday save the life of
your as-yet-unborn grandson?

01:02:36.480 --> 01:02:37.740 align:middle line:90%
And Harvey said, no.

01:02:37.740 --> 01:02:38.460 align:middle line:90%
He had no idea.

01:02:38.460 --> 01:02:41.040 align:middle line:84%
He said, I thought that if I
could do some cool science,

01:02:41.040 --> 01:02:43.880 align:middle line:84%
and help some patients,
that would be great.

01:02:43.880 --> 01:02:49.080 align:middle line:84%
1983 was two decades before
the human genome was sequenced.

01:02:49.080 --> 01:02:52.880 align:middle line:84%
So I had no idea that I
was carrying the mutation.

01:02:52.880 --> 01:02:57.520 align:middle line:84%
And in 2012, when
his son turned 10,

01:02:57.520 --> 01:03:00.750 align:middle line:84%
he developed the full-blown
symptoms of Gaucher's.

01:03:00.750 --> 01:03:04.230 align:middle line:84%
So he would be dead if
it weren't for the drug

01:03:04.230 --> 01:03:07.030 align:middle line:90%
that grandpa developed.

01:03:07.030 --> 01:03:09.470 align:middle line:84%
That's why I want
to be Harvey Lodish.

01:03:09.470 --> 01:03:13.090 align:middle line:84%
I don't have an MD or a
PhD in molecular biology.

01:03:13.090 --> 01:03:15.750 align:middle line:84%
I will never be able to
develop a drug that will save

01:03:15.750 --> 01:03:18.670 align:middle line:90%
my as-yet-unborn grandchildren.

01:03:18.670 --> 01:03:19.770 align:middle line:90%
But I realized something.

01:03:19.770 --> 01:03:21.210 align:middle line:90%
I realized that you know what?

01:03:21.210 --> 01:03:23.470 align:middle line:84%
We can all be
Harvey Lodish if we

01:03:23.470 --> 01:03:28.510 align:middle line:84%
invest in the drugs that save
our as-yet-unborn grandchildren.

01:03:28.510 --> 01:03:31.410 align:middle line:84%
Finance doesn't always
have to be a zero-sum game

01:03:31.410 --> 01:03:32.310 align:middle line:90%
if we don't let it.

01:03:32.310 --> 01:03:35.710 align:middle line:84%
With the right business
models, the right scale

01:03:35.710 --> 01:03:38.150 align:middle line:84%
at the right time,
we can do the thing

01:03:38.150 --> 01:03:41.510 align:middle line:84%
that everybody says they want
to do, but very few of us

01:03:41.510 --> 01:03:43.390 align:middle line:84%
ever get to do, which
is that we can actually

01:03:43.390 --> 01:03:45.510 align:middle line:90%
do well by doing good.

01:03:45.510 --> 01:03:48.087 align:middle line:84%
And with your help, we
can actually do this

01:03:48.087 --> 01:03:48.920 align:middle line:90%
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

