I'm sorry.

01:03:55.070 --> 01:03:58.020 align:middle line:90%
Thank you.

01:03:58.020 --> 01:03:59.510 align:middle line:90%
We have some time for questions?

01:03:59.510 --> 01:03:59.880 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: Sure.

01:03:59.880 --> 01:04:00.380 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:04:00.380 --> 01:04:03.260 align:middle line:84%
ANDREW W. LO: Yeah,
questions or comments?

01:04:03.260 --> 01:04:04.100 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:04:04.100 --> 01:04:05.940 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Yeah, [INAUDIBLE].

01:04:05.940 --> 01:04:08.500 align:middle line:84%
I know you mentioned
about when you're

01:04:08.500 --> 01:04:11.060 align:middle line:84%
talking about the
[INAUDIBLE] partners, when

01:04:11.060 --> 01:04:13.468 align:middle line:84%
you talk about the payout
from 10 years, 11, to 20.

01:04:13.468 --> 01:04:14.260 align:middle line:90%
ANDREW W. LO: Yeah.

01:04:14.260 --> 01:04:16.302 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: And you're saying
that that [INAUDIBLE]

01:04:16.302 --> 01:04:18.400 align:middle line:84%
is one month, how long
does a patent last.

01:04:18.400 --> 01:04:18.920 align:middle line:90%
ANDREW W. LO: That's right.

01:04:18.920 --> 01:04:20.712 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So I was
wondering after you said

01:04:20.712 --> 01:04:25.206 align:middle line:90%
that, other people [INAUDIBLE].

01:04:25.206 --> 01:04:28.760 align:middle line:84%
I guess I wasn't sure about
how that whole process works.

01:04:28.760 --> 01:04:35.948 align:middle line:90%
But after you [INAUDIBLE]

01:04:35.948 --> 01:04:36.740 align:middle line:90%
ANDREW W. LO: Sure.

01:04:36.740 --> 01:04:40.620 align:middle line:84%
So when you have a patent,
it gives you the right

01:04:40.620 --> 01:04:44.720 align:middle line:84%
to use the information in
the patent exclusively,

01:04:44.720 --> 01:04:49.100 align:middle line:84%
meaning nobody else is allowed
to use that information.

01:04:49.100 --> 01:04:51.760 align:middle line:84%
And so typically for drugs,
what is that information?

01:04:51.760 --> 01:04:53.980 align:middle line:84%
It's basically a
chemical formula

01:04:53.980 --> 01:04:56.330 align:middle line:84%
for a particular
compound that doesn't

01:04:56.330 --> 01:04:57.830 align:middle line:90%
exist in nature naturally.

01:04:57.830 --> 01:05:00.110 align:middle line:84%
You created it, you
came up with it,

01:05:00.110 --> 01:05:02.410 align:middle line:90%
you filed a patent disclosure.

01:05:02.410 --> 01:05:05.730 align:middle line:84%
And the patent office said,
yep, this is new, and useful.

01:05:05.730 --> 01:05:07.510 align:middle line:84%
We're going to grant
you the patent.

01:05:07.510 --> 01:05:11.810 align:middle line:84%
So for 20 years, only you
are allowed to use that idea.

01:05:11.810 --> 01:05:14.610 align:middle line:84%
And so when you use that
idea to create a drug,

01:05:14.610 --> 01:05:17.530 align:middle line:84%
you're the only one that's
allowed to make the drug.

01:05:17.530 --> 01:05:20.273 align:middle line:84%
And so if people want that
drug, they got to pay you.

01:05:20.273 --> 01:05:21.690 align:middle line:84%
And so you end up
charging a price

01:05:21.690 --> 01:05:25.930 align:middle line:84%
for the drug that will generate
a lot of value for you.

01:05:25.930 --> 01:05:28.850 align:middle line:84%
At the end of the 20
years, what's the deal?

01:05:28.850 --> 01:05:33.890 align:middle line:84%
The deal is everybody
gets to use that idea.

01:05:33.890 --> 01:05:37.090 align:middle line:84%
And so now, you've got companies
that can produce the drug.

01:05:37.090 --> 01:05:38.970 align:middle line:84%
Because typically, the
producing of the drug

01:05:38.970 --> 01:05:40.390 align:middle line:90%
is pretty cheap, right?

01:05:40.390 --> 01:05:43.010 align:middle line:84%
Once you give me the
formula, I can basically

01:05:43.010 --> 01:05:45.650 align:middle line:84%
get a chemical manufacturing
plant to come up with it

01:05:45.650 --> 01:05:49.610 align:middle line:84%
relatively cheaply,
a few pennies a pill.

01:05:49.610 --> 01:05:53.840 align:middle line:84%
The expensive part was
developing the clinical trials

01:05:53.840 --> 01:05:56.460 align:middle line:84%
to get the FDA to give
you the license to do it.

01:05:56.460 --> 01:06:00.360 align:middle line:84%
Once that's done, it's
all profits for you

01:06:00.360 --> 01:06:03.440 align:middle line:84%
until such time as
now everybody gets

01:06:03.440 --> 01:06:08.160 align:middle line:84%
to produce it for relatively
low amounts of money.

01:06:08.160 --> 01:06:09.840 align:middle line:90%
So it's like aspirin.

01:06:09.840 --> 01:06:12.700 align:middle line:84%
At one point,
aspirin was patented.

01:06:12.700 --> 01:06:16.927 align:middle line:84%
Now anybody can make it because
you could look up the patent.

01:06:16.927 --> 01:06:18.260 align:middle line:90%
There's the formula right there.

01:06:18.260 --> 01:06:21.640 align:middle line:84%
You can do it yourself if you
know how to mix those chemicals.

01:06:21.640 --> 01:06:23.380 align:middle line:84%
And you don't have to
pay anybody for it.

01:06:23.380 --> 01:06:26.120 align:middle line:84%
But before the patent
expires, you're

01:06:26.120 --> 01:06:27.340 align:middle line:90%
not allowed to use the idea.

01:06:27.340 --> 01:06:28.798 align:middle line:84%
Or if you want to
use the idea, you

01:06:28.798 --> 01:06:30.360 align:middle line:84%
have to pay the
owner of the patent

01:06:30.360 --> 01:06:32.880 align:middle line:90%
a fee for using their idea.

01:06:32.880 --> 01:06:35.640 align:middle line:90%
That's how it works.

01:06:35.640 --> 01:06:36.360 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:06:36.360 --> 01:06:39.080 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: How do you find
these investment opportunities?

01:06:39.080 --> 01:06:44.880 align:middle line:84%
How do you even begin to
research these [INAUDIBLE]?

01:06:44.880 --> 01:06:47.420 align:middle line:84%
ANDREW W. LO: Yeah, that's
a great, great question.

01:06:47.420 --> 01:06:50.870 align:middle line:84%
The answer is you need
to have expertise.

01:06:50.870 --> 01:06:56.470 align:middle line:84%
So as I mentioned, Neil Kumar,
the co-founder of BridgeBio,

01:06:56.470 --> 01:06:59.590 align:middle line:84%
and the person who
created it really,

01:06:59.590 --> 01:07:01.950 align:middle line:84%
Neil has a PhD in
chemical engineering.

01:07:01.950 --> 01:07:05.250 align:middle line:84%
But he didn't go off and
become a chemical engineer.

01:07:05.250 --> 01:07:08.150 align:middle line:84%
He went off and first
worked at McKinsey,

01:07:08.150 --> 01:07:11.830 align:middle line:84%
advising biotech companies
about their corporate strategy.

01:07:11.830 --> 01:07:14.590 align:middle line:84%
And ultimately, he
decided to start

01:07:14.590 --> 01:07:17.190 align:middle line:84%
investing in various
different ideas

01:07:17.190 --> 01:07:18.730 align:middle line:90%
because he had the expertise.

01:07:18.730 --> 01:07:22.210 align:middle line:84%
As a chemical engineer, you
have the science background,

01:07:22.210 --> 01:07:23.710 align:middle line:84%
the engineering
background, and then

01:07:23.710 --> 01:07:25.590 align:middle line:84%
as you work in the
industry, you learn

01:07:25.590 --> 01:07:26.810 align:middle line:90%
about how the industry works.

01:07:26.810 --> 01:07:28.690 align:middle line:90%
So you learn that aspect of it.

01:07:28.690 --> 01:07:30.350 align:middle line:84%
That's what gives
you the expertise.

01:07:30.350 --> 01:07:33.510 align:middle line:84%
And then once you have the
expertise, the companies that

01:07:33.510 --> 01:07:36.350 align:middle line:84%
have the money to invest,
the venture capital

01:07:36.350 --> 01:07:38.390 align:middle line:84%
firms, the private
equity firms, even

01:07:38.390 --> 01:07:41.550 align:middle line:84%
the big pharma companies
that have corporate venture

01:07:41.550 --> 01:07:44.670 align:middle line:84%
divisions, they will
want to hire you

01:07:44.670 --> 01:07:46.950 align:middle line:90%
to help them manage the money.

01:07:46.950 --> 01:07:50.390 align:middle line:84%
That's how you ultimately
end up being able to do that.

01:07:50.390 --> 01:07:51.640 align:middle line:90%
You got to have the expertise.

01:07:51.640 --> 01:07:53.515 align:middle line:84%
And by the way, that's
one of the reasons why

01:07:53.515 --> 01:07:56.940 align:middle line:84%
MIT graduates are so popular
among Wall Street investment

01:07:56.940 --> 01:08:00.340 align:middle line:84%
houses, because you all
not only have expertise,

01:08:00.340 --> 01:08:03.820 align:middle line:84%
but you have the ability
to learn and develop

01:08:03.820 --> 01:08:05.900 align:middle line:90%
new expertise as needed.

01:08:05.900 --> 01:08:09.740 align:middle line:90%
That's really important.

01:08:09.740 --> 01:08:10.442 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

01:08:10.442 --> 01:08:11.900 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: How do
we invest in this?

01:08:11.900 --> 01:08:12.560 align:middle line:90%
ANDREW W. LO: What's that.

01:08:12.560 --> 01:08:14.020 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: How do
we invest in this?

01:08:14.020 --> 01:08:15.340 align:middle line:90%
ANDREW W. LO: How do you invest?

01:08:15.340 --> 01:08:17.298 align:middle line:84%
Well, that's actually
the same question I have.

01:08:17.298 --> 01:08:19.220 align:middle line:90%
How do I invest in you?

01:08:19.220 --> 01:08:23.520 align:middle line:84%
You all are the next generation
of investment opportunities.

01:08:23.520 --> 01:08:25.899 align:middle line:84%
And I'm not saying this
just to flatter you.

01:08:25.899 --> 01:08:28.060 align:middle line:90%
This is a biological fact.

01:08:28.060 --> 01:08:31.620 align:middle line:84%
You are going to be around a lot
longer than Jake, Peter, and I.

01:08:31.620 --> 01:08:35.420 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, all of you
are the future of innovation.

01:08:35.420 --> 01:08:36.680 align:middle line:90%
And so, yes.

01:08:36.680 --> 01:08:38.580 align:middle line:84%
I would love to figure
out how you invest.

01:08:38.580 --> 01:08:41.460 align:middle line:84%
In terms of investing in
these kind of companies,

01:08:41.460 --> 01:08:44.160 align:middle line:84%
the biotech companies, well,
BridgeBio is publicly traded.

01:08:44.160 --> 01:08:45.120 align:middle line:90%
So anybody can invest.

01:08:45.120 --> 01:08:48.170 align:middle line:84%
And I'm not suggesting
you should invest in them.

01:08:48.170 --> 01:08:50.890 align:middle line:84%
There's risk, very, very
high risk, by the way,

01:08:50.890 --> 01:08:51.990 align:middle line:90%
in biotech companies.

01:08:51.990 --> 01:08:54.529 align:middle line:84%
So you need to be very
careful about that.

01:08:54.529 --> 01:08:56.850 align:middle line:84%
But the much harder
areas to invest

01:08:56.850 --> 01:09:01.050 align:middle line:84%
are the private companies,
because by definition, they're

01:09:01.050 --> 01:09:01.550 align:middle line:90%
private.

01:09:01.550 --> 01:09:03.050 align:middle line:84%
So you don't know
as much about them

01:09:03.050 --> 01:09:04.890 align:middle line:84%
as publicly-traded
companies where

01:09:04.890 --> 01:09:08.450 align:middle line:84%
you've got a lot of
published information.

01:09:08.450 --> 01:09:11.990 align:middle line:84%
And you probably are already
aware, but if you're not,

01:09:11.990 --> 01:09:17.609 align:middle line:84%
you should be, that MIT is an
incredibly attractive place

01:09:17.609 --> 01:09:20.569 align:middle line:84%
for private investors
to come and look

01:09:20.569 --> 01:09:24.970 align:middle line:84%
for opportunities among the
students and the faculty.

01:09:24.970 --> 01:09:27.490 align:middle line:84%
You are in a wonderful
position, particularly

01:09:27.490 --> 01:09:29.390 align:middle line:84%
those of you who are
majoring in math,

01:09:29.390 --> 01:09:33.010 align:middle line:84%
because your skills are portable
to virtually every industry

01:09:33.010 --> 01:09:33.510 align:middle line:90%
out there.

01:09:33.510 --> 01:09:37.330 align:middle line:84%
I don't know of an industry
that doesn't need math.

01:09:37.330 --> 01:09:39.298 align:middle line:84%
Maybe the Hollywood
film industry.

01:09:39.298 --> 01:09:41.090 align:middle line:84%
But you've got to count
your money somehow.

01:09:41.090 --> 01:09:42.590 align:middle line:84%
Tom Cruise has to
count his money.

01:09:42.590 --> 01:09:44.470 align:middle line:84%
So you got to have
math for that.

01:09:44.470 --> 01:09:49.069 align:middle line:84%
So I think that there are lots
of venture capitalists hanging

01:09:49.069 --> 01:09:52.750 align:middle line:84%
around Kendall Square and
MIT to try to understand

01:09:52.750 --> 01:09:55.150 align:middle line:90%
whether they can invest in you.

01:09:55.150 --> 01:09:58.803 align:middle line:84%
You will find that to be a very,
very interesting phenomenon.

01:09:58.803 --> 01:09:59.970 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, I'll come back to you.

01:09:59.970 --> 01:10:01.420 align:middle line:90%
But you were first.

01:10:01.420 --> 01:10:04.010 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] thing
that you want to invest in,

01:10:04.010 --> 01:10:07.577 align:middle line:84%
these groups already exist that
are trying to use these things,

01:10:07.577 --> 01:10:08.410 align:middle line:90%
but they need money.

01:10:08.410 --> 01:10:09.830 align:middle line:84%
Or is it that the
investment going

01:10:09.830 --> 01:10:14.133 align:middle line:84%
towards starting XYZ that
does this specific thing?

01:10:14.133 --> 01:10:15.550 align:middle line:84%
ANDREW W. LO: Well,
there is still

01:10:15.550 --> 01:10:17.950 align:middle line:84%
a valley of death in
the sense that there

01:10:17.950 --> 01:10:20.830 align:middle line:84%
are lots of groups
that want money

01:10:20.830 --> 01:10:23.350 align:middle line:84%
to do the kind of cool
things that you are all going

01:10:23.350 --> 01:10:25.470 align:middle line:90%
to come up with on your own.

01:10:25.470 --> 01:10:27.930 align:middle line:84%
By the way, to put in a
plug for Dennis Whyte,

01:10:27.930 --> 01:10:31.215 align:middle line:84%
he teaches a course on
nuclear engineering,

01:10:31.215 --> 01:10:32.590 align:middle line:84%
where as part of
the course, they

01:10:32.590 --> 01:10:34.630 align:middle line:84%
come up with ideas,
a number of which

01:10:34.630 --> 01:10:37.110 align:middle line:84%
ultimately end up becoming
commercialized and real

01:10:37.110 --> 01:10:38.150 align:middle line:90%
startups.

01:10:38.150 --> 01:10:41.350 align:middle line:84%
So there are a
number of companies

01:10:41.350 --> 01:10:44.580 align:middle line:84%
that can't get funded because
of the valley of death.

01:10:44.580 --> 01:10:47.100 align:middle line:84%
MIT has a much
better opportunity

01:10:47.100 --> 01:10:51.140 align:middle line:84%
set than if you were, say, at
Duke University or University

01:10:51.140 --> 01:10:52.460 align:middle line:90%
of North Carolina.

01:10:52.460 --> 01:10:56.020 align:middle line:84%
That's part of the research
triangle down south.

01:10:56.020 --> 01:10:58.700 align:middle line:84%
And they've got some really
cool ideas, smart students,

01:10:58.700 --> 01:10:59.680 align:middle line:90%
and faculty as well.

01:10:59.680 --> 01:11:02.640 align:middle line:84%
But because they're not part of
the Boston, Cambridge ecosystem,

01:11:02.640 --> 01:11:04.890 align:middle line:84%
they don't have as many
venture capitalists down there

01:11:04.890 --> 01:11:07.260 align:middle line:84%
looking to fund various
different projects.

01:11:07.260 --> 01:11:09.740 align:middle line:84%
So I would say that
there are still

01:11:09.740 --> 01:11:13.700 align:middle line:84%
lots and lots of smaller groups
that are looking for funding

01:11:13.700 --> 01:11:16.460 align:middle line:84%
that can't get it because
they just don't know enough

01:11:16.460 --> 01:11:17.940 align:middle line:90%
about the financing.

01:11:17.940 --> 01:11:21.500 align:middle line:84%
So what I've described to you
now, in just this one class,

01:11:21.500 --> 01:11:26.060 align:middle line:84%
is probably more information
than most startup groups have

01:11:26.060 --> 01:11:28.060 align:middle line:84%
in terms of thinking
about risk rewards,

01:11:28.060 --> 01:11:31.140 align:middle line:90%
and trade offs like that.

01:11:31.140 --> 01:11:32.033 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

01:11:32.033 --> 01:11:34.200 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE],
you're also a professor here.

01:11:34.200 --> 01:11:41.450 align:middle line:84%
So I was wondering [INAUDIBLE]
