It's not part of OCW, but
Open Learning Library.

01:12:17.370 --> 01:12:18.190 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:12:18.190 --> 01:12:21.410 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So the gentleman
who started [INAUDIBLE],

01:12:21.410 --> 01:12:24.530 align:middle line:84%
can you just give a rough
outline of how on Earth he

01:12:24.530 --> 01:12:25.590 align:middle line:90%
did that from a PhD?

01:12:25.590 --> 01:12:28.890 align:middle line:90%
[INAUDIBLE]

01:12:28.890 --> 01:12:29.690 align:middle line:90%
ANDREW W. LO: Sure.

01:12:29.690 --> 01:12:32.290 align:middle line:84%
So the first thing he did
when he graduated was he

01:12:32.290 --> 01:12:34.610 align:middle line:84%
went to McKinsey, a
consulting company.

01:12:34.610 --> 01:12:36.578 align:middle line:84%
And he knew a lot of
science and engineering,

01:12:36.578 --> 01:12:38.370 align:middle line:84%
but he didn't know much
about the industry.

01:12:38.370 --> 01:12:43.800 align:middle line:84%
As part of McKinsey's program,
they train their hires

01:12:43.800 --> 01:12:46.680 align:middle line:90%
to learn about the industry.

01:12:46.680 --> 01:12:49.440 align:middle line:84%
So probably for
the first year, he

01:12:49.440 --> 01:12:53.320 align:middle line:84%
shadowed a more experienced
biopharma consultant

01:12:53.320 --> 01:12:55.960 align:middle line:84%
that brought him into
various different companies

01:12:55.960 --> 01:12:58.800 align:middle line:84%
and showed him projects
that they were working on,

01:12:58.800 --> 01:13:02.260 align:middle line:84%
and how he could add to those
projects by doing research.

01:13:02.260 --> 01:13:05.160 align:middle line:84%
Just like if he were a chemical
engineer doing research

01:13:05.160 --> 01:13:08.080 align:middle line:84%
on better ways of
refining oil, he'd

01:13:08.080 --> 01:13:10.480 align:middle line:84%
have to go and read articles,
and then talk to people,

01:13:10.480 --> 01:13:11.720 align:middle line:90%
and then do experiments.

01:13:11.720 --> 01:13:14.840 align:middle line:84%
In that same way, if you're in
a consulting firm like McKinsey,

01:13:14.840 --> 01:13:17.660 align:middle line:84%
and you're working
on a project like,

01:13:17.660 --> 01:13:23.062 align:middle line:84%
how should Pfizer fund their
vaccine program for RSV?

01:13:23.062 --> 01:13:24.020 align:middle line:90%
Well, what would he do?

01:13:24.020 --> 01:13:26.437 align:middle line:84%
He would go and learn about
RSV, he would read the journal

01:13:26.437 --> 01:13:29.062 align:middle line:84%
articles, he would figure out
how it's being funded now,

01:13:29.062 --> 01:13:30.520 align:middle line:84%
he would come up
with new ideas, he

01:13:30.520 --> 01:13:33.480 align:middle line:84%
would try to test them out by
writing different simulation

01:13:33.480 --> 01:13:35.500 align:middle line:84%
examples to illustrate
what could happen.

01:13:35.500 --> 01:13:38.000 align:middle line:84%
Those are the kind of things
that he did for the first three

01:13:38.000 --> 01:13:40.270 align:middle line:90%
to five years of his career.

01:13:40.270 --> 01:13:42.590 align:middle line:84%
At some point, he learned
enough about the industry

01:13:42.590 --> 01:13:45.990 align:middle line:84%
to start making
investment decisions,

01:13:45.990 --> 01:13:48.790 align:middle line:84%
like, this company has got a
good bet, because all the things

01:13:48.790 --> 01:13:51.350 align:middle line:84%
that I was studying about
proper ways of funding

01:13:51.350 --> 01:13:53.902 align:middle line:84%
particular projects, they're
doing it versus this company,

01:13:53.902 --> 01:13:56.110 align:middle line:84%
I don't really want to invest
because they don't even

01:13:56.110 --> 01:13:58.950 align:middle line:84%
understand how to fund
their internal pipeline.

01:13:58.950 --> 01:14:01.630 align:middle line:84%
So at some point,
he left McKinsey

01:14:01.630 --> 01:14:06.110 align:middle line:84%
to join Third Rock Ventures,
which is a biotech venture

01:14:06.110 --> 01:14:07.237 align:middle line:90%
capital company.

01:14:07.237 --> 01:14:09.070 align:middle line:84%
And then he spent a few
years there learning

01:14:09.070 --> 01:14:11.110 align:middle line:90%
how to make investments.

01:14:11.110 --> 01:14:14.810 align:middle line:84%
It was at Third Rock that he
approached me and said, look,

01:14:14.810 --> 01:14:17.910 align:middle line:84%
I've been thinking about
rare diseases as well.

01:14:17.910 --> 01:14:20.870 align:middle line:84%
I've seen investments
that Third Rock

01:14:20.870 --> 01:14:25.690 align:middle line:84%
has passed on because it's not
a so-called platform technology.

01:14:25.690 --> 01:14:31.430 align:middle line:84%
It's only a one-trick pony, one
idea, one drug, one disease.

01:14:31.430 --> 01:14:34.270 align:middle line:84%
And Third Rock thought
it was too risky.

01:14:34.270 --> 01:14:37.700 align:middle line:84%
Would you be willing to
run some models with me

01:14:37.700 --> 01:14:39.900 align:middle line:90%
to fund those projects?

01:14:39.900 --> 01:14:42.800 align:middle line:84%
And after we ran a
bunch of simulations,

01:14:42.800 --> 01:14:45.140 align:middle line:84%
it gave him enough
conviction that this

01:14:45.140 --> 01:14:47.460 align:middle line:84%
was going to be a
profitable venture that he

01:14:47.460 --> 01:14:50.200 align:middle line:84%
was willing to take the
risk to try to do that.

01:14:50.200 --> 01:14:52.180 align:middle line:84%
And the first
thing we did was we

01:14:52.180 --> 01:14:54.340 align:middle line:84%
asked Third Rock,
you guys passed

01:14:54.340 --> 01:14:56.340 align:middle line:90%
on these three opportunities.

01:14:56.340 --> 01:14:57.860 align:middle line:90%
Do you mind if we take them?

01:14:57.860 --> 01:14:59.160 align:middle line:90%
Can we invest in them?

01:14:59.160 --> 01:15:00.400 align:middle line:90%
Third Rock said, be my guest.

01:15:00.400 --> 01:15:01.500 align:middle line:90%
Go for it.

01:15:01.500 --> 01:15:04.920 align:middle line:84%
And what was not
appropriate for Third Rock,

01:15:04.920 --> 01:15:07.100 align:middle line:84%
given they've got lots
of other opportunities

01:15:07.100 --> 01:15:10.980 align:middle line:84%
that they're focusing on,
was a great opportunity

01:15:10.980 --> 01:15:14.140 align:middle line:84%
set for BridgeBio,
this portfolio company.

01:15:14.140 --> 01:15:17.260 align:middle line:84%
So like anything else, it's
a gradual process where you

01:15:17.260 --> 01:15:18.840 align:middle line:90%
learn more, and more, and more.

01:15:18.840 --> 01:15:20.780 align:middle line:84%
And sometimes, at
some point, you

01:15:20.780 --> 01:15:23.032 align:middle line:84%
learn so much, that you
are confident that you

01:15:23.032 --> 01:15:25.762 align:middle line:84%
know enough to be able to
start your own company.

01:15:25.762 --> 01:15:26.720 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: That's great.

01:15:26.720 --> 01:15:26.980 align:middle line:90%
Thank you so much.

01:15:26.980 --> 01:15:28.060 align:middle line:90%
ANDREW W. LO: Yeah, sure.

01:15:28.060 --> 01:15:30.580 align:middle line:84%
By the way, it doesn't
mean it's any less scary.

01:15:30.580 --> 01:15:32.580 align:middle line:84%
There is still a
scary moment where

01:15:32.580 --> 01:15:34.610 align:middle line:84%
you've learned all
this stuff, but still,

01:15:34.610 --> 01:15:39.630 align:middle line:84%
quitting your job with a baby
at home, and bills to pay,

01:15:39.630 --> 01:15:40.590 align:middle line:90%
that is scary.

01:15:40.590 --> 01:15:44.810 align:middle line:84%
But at some point, your
confidence and your ability

01:15:44.810 --> 01:15:49.810 align:middle line:84%
to get through that scary
period outweighs the wall

01:15:49.810 --> 01:15:54.090 align:middle line:90%
that your emotions put up.

01:15:54.090 --> 01:15:54.890 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

01:15:54.890 --> 01:15:59.090 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I have a question
about in times of crisis

01:15:59.090 --> 01:16:01.730 align:middle line:84%
or when you actually
need some type of growth,

01:16:01.730 --> 01:16:07.530 align:middle line:84%
or for example like a COVID-19
vaccine or you need [INAUDIBLE].

01:16:07.530 --> 01:16:10.650 align:middle line:90%


01:16:10.650 --> 01:16:13.170 align:middle line:84%
I guess, because you talked
about the [INAUDIBLE] and all

01:16:13.170 --> 01:16:13.670 align:middle line:90%
that stuff.

01:16:13.670 --> 01:16:16.810 align:middle line:90%
Is that during those times?

01:16:16.810 --> 01:16:18.110 align:middle line:90%
How is it adjusted?

01:16:18.110 --> 01:16:24.610 align:middle line:84%
I would say, or if you can get
into how it's like [INAUDIBLE].

01:16:24.610 --> 01:16:29.770 align:middle line:84%
ANDREW W. LO: Well, so that's
a really important observation,

01:16:29.770 --> 01:16:34.057 align:middle line:84%
because in the case of COVID-19,
we did have a valley of death.

01:16:34.057 --> 01:16:35.640 align:middle line:84%
And we still do have
a valley of death

01:16:35.640 --> 01:16:38.360 align:middle line:84%
when it comes to vaccines,
because the profitability

01:16:38.360 --> 01:16:41.400 align:middle line:84%
of the typical vaccine
is not very attractive.

01:16:41.400 --> 01:16:45.000 align:middle line:84%
And so prior to COVID,
most big pharma companies

01:16:45.000 --> 01:16:47.360 align:middle line:84%
were actually getting out
of the vaccine business.

01:16:47.360 --> 01:16:49.480 align:middle line:90%
Cancer was much more profitable.

01:16:49.480 --> 01:16:52.880 align:middle line:84%
Rare diseases actually ended
up being more profitable

01:16:52.880 --> 01:16:54.080 align:middle line:90%
than vaccines.

01:16:54.080 --> 01:16:57.660 align:middle line:84%
And so right before
the pandemic hit,

01:16:57.660 --> 01:17:01.000 align:middle line:84%
there were only four big
pharma companies left

01:17:01.000 --> 01:17:04.440 align:middle line:84%
that were still in the
vaccine-making business.

01:17:04.440 --> 01:17:07.680 align:middle line:84%
And but once the
pandemic hit, and we

01:17:07.680 --> 01:17:11.520 align:middle line:84%
knew that it was going to be
bad, when it came on US shores

01:17:11.520 --> 01:17:15.120 align:middle line:84%
in March of 2020, we knew
it was going to be bad.

01:17:15.120 --> 01:17:16.960 align:middle line:84%
At that point, a lot
of things started

01:17:16.960 --> 01:17:18.800 align:middle line:90%
to happen all at the same time.

01:17:18.800 --> 01:17:21.120 align:middle line:84%
First of all,
philanthropy had been

01:17:21.120 --> 01:17:25.060 align:middle line:84%
funding scientific experiments
for vaccines for decades,

01:17:25.060 --> 01:17:27.000 align:middle line:84%
in particular, the
Gates Foundation.

01:17:27.000 --> 01:17:29.390 align:middle line:84%
They, for years,
have been working

01:17:29.390 --> 01:17:31.830 align:middle line:90%
on a vaccine for malaria.

01:17:31.830 --> 01:17:33.170 align:middle line:90%
Still doesn't exist, by the way.

01:17:33.170 --> 01:17:35.712 align:middle line:84%
We still don't have a vaccine
for malaria, believe it or not.

01:17:35.712 --> 01:17:39.010 align:middle line:84%
Malaria is probably the single
biggest killer around the world.

01:17:39.010 --> 01:17:41.670 align:middle line:84%
Not here in the
US, but in Africa,

01:17:41.670 --> 01:17:44.890 align:middle line:84%
where malaria is a real problem
for many, many countries.

01:17:44.890 --> 01:17:47.830 align:middle line:84%
And it kills literally
millions of people every year.

01:17:47.830 --> 01:17:50.770 align:middle line:84%
So the Gates Foundation had been
working on that for many years,

01:17:50.770 --> 01:17:54.970 align:middle line:84%
funding scientific research, and
funding Moderna in particular,

01:17:54.970 --> 01:17:56.830 align:middle line:90%
among other companies.

01:17:56.830 --> 01:17:59.990 align:middle line:84%
And so once the
vaccine hit US soil,

01:17:59.990 --> 01:18:03.150 align:middle line:84%
we were very, very quick
to be able to collaborate

01:18:03.150 --> 01:18:05.450 align:middle line:84%
with international
scientists to get

01:18:05.450 --> 01:18:07.510 align:middle line:84%
the sequence, the
genetic sequence,

01:18:07.510 --> 01:18:09.510 align:middle line:84%
of the particular spike
protein that we're

01:18:09.510 --> 01:18:12.630 align:middle line:84%
going to target with the
various different antibodies.

01:18:12.630 --> 01:18:16.750 align:middle line:84%
So that process
worked like magic.

01:18:16.750 --> 01:18:19.750 align:middle line:84%
There was no valley of death
for that period of time,

01:18:19.750 --> 01:18:21.708 align:middle line:84%
because the government
stepped in and said,

01:18:21.708 --> 01:18:23.750 align:middle line:84%
we got to do something,
otherwise, lots of people

01:18:23.750 --> 01:18:25.430 align:middle line:90%
are going to die.

01:18:25.430 --> 01:18:29.250 align:middle line:84%
For the very first time in
the history of the industry,

01:18:29.250 --> 01:18:40.250 align:middle line:84%
it took 63 days from the time
we sequenced the COVID-19 virus

01:18:40.250 --> 01:18:44.770 align:middle line:84%
to when the first human patient
was injected with the vaccine

01:18:44.770 --> 01:18:46.970 align:middle line:90%
candidate, 63 days.

01:18:46.970 --> 01:18:52.330 align:middle line:84%
Typically, it takes three to
five years to create a vaccine.

01:18:52.330 --> 01:18:54.290 align:middle line:90%
It took us 63 days.

01:18:54.290 --> 01:18:57.290 align:middle line:84%
That's an example of what you're
talking about getting around

01:18:57.290 --> 01:18:58.450 align:middle line:90%
the valley of death.

01:18:58.450 --> 01:19:03.710 align:middle line:84%
The problem is now that COVID
seems like it's over, of course,

01:19:03.710 --> 01:19:04.950 align:middle line:90%
it's not really over.

01:19:04.950 --> 01:19:06.770 align:middle line:84%
You can still get
COVID-19, as many of you

01:19:06.770 --> 01:19:08.810 align:middle line:84%
will find out in
the next few months.

01:19:08.810 --> 01:19:09.863 align:middle line:90%
But it's not as serious.

01:19:09.863 --> 01:19:11.030 align:middle line:90%
We know how to deal with it.

01:19:11.030 --> 01:19:13.430 align:middle line:84%
We've got a number of
drugs and treatments.

01:19:13.430 --> 01:19:16.090 align:middle line:84%
And we recognize the
various different symptoms.

01:19:16.090 --> 01:19:20.330 align:middle line:84%
So because it is
not that serious,

01:19:20.330 --> 01:19:21.870 align:middle line:84%
people are more
relaxed about it.

01:19:21.870 --> 01:19:24.045 align:middle line:84%
And what that means is the
valley of death is back.

01:19:24.045 --> 01:19:25.670 align:middle line:84%
People don't want to
spend money on it.

01:19:25.670 --> 01:19:27.740 align:middle line:84%
They'd rather spend
money on, guess what?

01:19:27.740 --> 01:19:28.520 align:middle line:90%
AI.

01:19:28.520 --> 01:19:29.720 align:middle line:90%
Right?

01:19:29.720 --> 01:19:33.200 align:middle line:84%
Let's put money in OpenAI,
or Anthropic, or any one

01:19:33.200 --> 01:19:34.840 align:middle line:90%
of these other companies.

01:19:34.840 --> 01:19:37.547 align:middle line:90%
And that's a problem.

01:19:37.547 --> 01:19:39.380 align:middle line:84%
So I think that we need
to worry about that.

01:19:39.380 --> 01:19:41.400 align:middle line:84%
That's one of the reasons
why I'm here today,

01:19:41.400 --> 01:19:43.920 align:middle line:84%
to try to get some of you
to be excited about taking

01:19:43.920 --> 01:19:48.220 align:middle line:84%
your ideas, and your toolkits,
and doing well by doing good.

01:19:48.220 --> 01:19:50.572 align:middle line:84%
You will really do
spectacularly well,

01:19:50.572 --> 01:19:52.280 align:middle line:84%
from a financial point
of view, if you're

01:19:52.280 --> 01:19:56.600 align:middle line:84%
able to succeed in fusion, or
in drug development devices,

01:19:56.600 --> 01:19:57.560 align:middle line:90%
and so on.

01:19:57.560 --> 01:19:59.517 align:middle line:84%
But even more
importantly, you're

01:19:59.517 --> 01:20:01.600 align:middle line:84%
going to feel really good
about what you're doing.

01:20:01.600 --> 01:20:04.240 align:middle line:84%
And you're going to actually
help a lot of people.

01:20:04.240 --> 01:20:04.740 align:middle line:90%
All right.

01:20:04.740 --> 01:20:05.407 align:middle line:90%
I'm out of time.

01:20:05.407 --> 01:20:07.560 align:middle line:84%
So thank you very much,
and have a good day.

01:20:07.560 --> 01:20:09.390 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Thank you.

01:20:09.390 --> 01:30:06.000 align:middle line:90%